      "text": "<|begin_of_thought|>\n\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 19 and 12. Hmm, let me think. Multiplying two numbers together. Let me recall how to do that. I remember that when you multiply two numbers, you can break them down into smaller parts. Maybe I can use the distributive property here. \n\nThe distributive property says that a*(b + c) = a*b + a*c. So, if I can express 19 as 10 + 9, then maybe I can use that. Let me try that. \n\nSo, 19 times 12 is the same as (10 + 9) times 12. Then, I can apply the distributive property again. That would be 10 times 12 plus 9 times 12. \n\nCalculating each part separately. 10 times 12 is straightforward. 10 times 10 is 100, and 10 times 2 is 20. So, 100 + 20 is 120. \n\nThen, 9 times 12. Let me compute that. 9 times 10 is 90, and 9 times 2 is 18. Adding those together gives 90 + 18 = 108. \n\nNow, adding the two results together: 120 + 108. Let me do that. 120 + 100 is 220, and then 108 is 22 more. So, 220 + 22 is 242. \n\nWait, let me check if that's right. Let me multiply 19 and 12 again another way to verify. Maybe using another method. \n\nAnother way is to use the standard multiplication algorithm. Let's write 19 and 12 vertically. \n\n  19\n\u00d7 12\n-----\nMultiply 19 by 2: 38\nMultiply 19 by 10: 190\nAdd them together: 38 + 190 = 228. \n\nHmm, that's the same result as before. So, 228. \n\nAlternatively, maybe using the fact that 19 is 10 + 9. So, 10*12 + 9*12. Which is 120 + 108 = 228. \n\nAll methods lead to the same answer. So, I can be confident that the product of 19 and 12 is 228. \n\nWait, let me just confirm once more. 19 times 10 is 190, and 19 times 2 is 38. Adding them gives 228. Yep, that's correct. \n\nI don't think I made any mistakes here. The distributive property worked out nicely, and the standard multiplication also gave the same result. So, I think that's solid. \n\nJust to recap: 19*12. Break 19 into 10+9. Multiply 10*12=120, 9*12=108. Add them: 120+108=228. Yep, that's the answer. \n\n<|end_of_thought|>\n\n<|begin_of_solution|>\n\nThe product of 19 and 12 is calculated as follows:\n\n1. **Break down 19 into 10 + 9**:  \n   \\[\n   19 \\times 12 = (10 + 9) \\times 12\n   \\]\n\n2. **Apply the distributive property**:  \n   \\[\n   10 \\times 12 + 9 \\times 12\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate each part**:  \n   - \\(10 \\times 12 = 120\\)\n   - \\(9 \\times 12 = 108\\)\n\n4. **Add the results**:  \n   \\[\n   120 + 108 = 228\n   \\]\n\n**Verification**:  \n- Using the standard multiplication algorithm:  \n  \\[\n  19 \\times 12 = 228\n  \\]\n\n**Alternative method**:  \n- \\(19 = 10 + 9\\), so  \n  \\[\n  (10 + 9) \\times 12 = 10 \\times 12 + 9 \\times 12 = 120 + 108 = 228\n  \\]\n\nBoth methods confirm the product is \\(\\boxed{228}\\).\n\n<|end_of_solution|>",
