      "index": 0,
      "text": "<|begin_of_thought|>\n\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 25 and 36. Hmm, let me think. Multiplying two numbers together. I remember that when you multiply a number by 10, you just add a zero to the end. But here, one of the numbers is 25, which is 25, and the other is 36. Let me break this down step by step.\n\nFirst, let's look at 25. If I multiply 25 by 10, that would be 250. But since the question is asking for the product of 25 and 36, which is 10 times 36, maybe I can use that. Let me verify that. 10 times 36 is 360. So, 25 times 36 is 360. Wait, that seems straightforward. But let me check if there's another way to approach this.\n\nAlternatively, I can use the distributive property. Multiplying 25 by 36 can be broken down into 25 times 30 plus 25 times 6. Let me compute each part separately. 25 times 30 is 750, and 25 times 6 is 150. Adding those together gives 750 + 150 = 900. So, 25 times 36 is 900. That's another way to get the same answer. So, both methods confirm that the product is 900.\n\nWait, but let me make sure I didn't make any mistakes. Let's take 25 times 30 first. 25 times 30 is indeed 750. Then 25 times 6 is 150. Adding them gives 900. That seems right. Alternatively, if I do 25 times 36, maybe I can think of it as 25 times 30 plus 25 times 6. 25 times 30 is 750, 25 times 6 is 150. 750 + 150 is 900. Yep, that's correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the standard multiplication algorithm. Let's write 25 and 36 vertically. Multiply 25 by 6 first. 25 times 6 is 150. Then multiply 25 by 30, which is 750. Adding those two results together: 150 + 750 = 900. Same answer again. So, all methods lead to the same result. Therefore, the product of 25 and 36 is 900.\n\nI think that's solid. Let me just recap to make sure I didn't skip any steps. The problem is straightforward multiplication. Breaking it down into smaller parts using the distributive property or the standard algorithm both confirm the answer. There's no trick here; it's just basic multiplication. So, I can be confident that 25 times 36 is indeed 900.\n\n<|end_of_thought|>\n\n<|begin_of_solution|>\n\nThe product of \\(25\\) and \\(36\\) is calculated as follows:\n\n1. **Breakdown using distributive property**:\n   \\[\n   25 \\times 36 = 25 \\times (30 + 6) = 25 \\times 30 + 25 \\times 6\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   25 \\times 30 = 750 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 25 \\times 6 = 150\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   750 + 150 = 900\n   \\]\n\n2. **Direct multiplication**:\n   \\[\n   25 \\times 30 = 750 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 25 \\times 6 = 150\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   750 + 150 = 900\n   \\]\n\nBoth methods confirm the result. The product of \\(25\\) and \\(36\\) is \\(\\boxed{900}\\).\n\n<|end_of_solution|>",
