      "text": "<|begin_of_thought|>\n\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 80 and 19. Hmm, let me think. Multiplying two numbers together. Let me recall how to do this. I remember that when multiplying, you can break down one of the numbers into smaller parts to make the multiplication easier. Maybe I can use the distributive property here. \n\nLet me write down the numbers: 80 and 19. If I break down 80 into 80 and 10, that might help. Because 10 times 19 is a simpler multiplication. Let me check that: 10 times 19 is 190. Then, 80 times 19 would be 80 times 19. But 80 times 19 is the same as 80 times (10 + 9), which is 80*10 + 80*9. \n\nCalculating each part: 80*10 is 800, and 80*9 is 720. Adding those together: 800 + 720. Let me add those. 800 + 700 is 1500, and then 20 more is 1520. So 80*19 is 1520. \n\nWait, let me verify that. Maybe I can do it another way. Let's see, 80*19. If I think of 80 as 8*10, then 8*10 is 80. Then, 8*19. Let's compute 8*19. 8*10 is 80, and 8*9 is 72. So 80 + 72 is 152. So that's another way to get 152. \n\nSo both methods give me 152. Let me check if there's a different approach. Maybe using the standard multiplication algorithm. Let's write 80 and 19 vertically. \n\nFirst, multiply 80 by 9. 80*9 is 720. Then, multiply 80 by 10, which is 800. Then add those two results together: 720 + 800. That's 1520. Yep, same answer. \n\nAlternatively, if I use the distributive property again, 80*(10 + 9) = 80*10 + 80*9 = 800 + 720 = 1520. \n\nI think all these methods confirm that the product of 80 and 19 is 1520. Let me just make sure there's no mistake in any of the steps. \n\nFirst, breaking down 80 into 80 and 10. That's correct. Then, 80*19 is indeed 80*(10 + 9) = 80*10 + 80*9. Then, 80*10 is straightforward. Then, 80*9 is 720. Adding 720 to 800 gives 1520. \n\nAlternatively, 80*19. If I do 80*10 first, that's 800. Then 80*9 is 720. Adding them gives 1520. \n\nAnother way: 80*(10 + 9) = 80*10 + 80*9 = 800 + 720 = 1520. \n\nAll methods lead to the same result. So I'm confident that the product of 80 and 19 is 1520. \n\n<|end_of_thought|>\n\n<|begin_of_solution|>\n\nThe product of 80 and 19 is calculated as follows:\n\n1. **Break down 80 into 80 and 10**:  \n   \\( 80 \\times 19 = 80 \\times (10 + 9) = 80 \\times 10 + 80 \\times 9 \\).\n\n2. **Compute each part**:  \n   - \\( 80 \\times 10 = 800 \\)  \n   - \\( 80 \\times 9 = 720 \\)  \n   - Adding these results: \\( 800 + 720 = 1520 \\).\n\n3. **Verification using another method**:  \n   - \\( 80 \\times 19 = 80 \\times (10 + 9) = 80 \\times 10 + 80 \\times 9 = 800 + 720 = 1520 \\).\n\nBoth methods confirm the product is \\(\\boxed{1520}\\).\n\n<|end_of_solution|>",
