      "index": 0,
      "text": "<|begin_of_thought|>\n\nOkay, let's see. The question is asking for the product of 10 and 34. Hmm, product... I remember that in math, the product of two numbers is the result when you multiply them together. So, basically, I need to multiply 10 by 34. Let me think about how to do this.\n\nFirst, maybe I can break down one of the numbers into smaller parts to make the multiplication easier. Let's take 34. 34 is 30 plus 4. So, if I can split 34 into 30 and 4, then multiply 10 by 30 and 10 by 4, and then add those two results together. That might be a good approach.\n\nLet me write that out. 10 multiplied by 30 is 300, and 10 multiplied by 4 is 40. Then adding those two products together: 300 + 40. That would be 340. So, 10 times 34 is 340. Let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method. Let's see, 10 times 30 is definitely 300, and 10 times 4 is 40. Adding those gives 340. That seems right. But let me verify with another multiplication method to be thorough.\n\nAnother way is to use the distributive property. 10 times 34 is the same as 10 times (30 + 4). So, 10 times 30 is 300, and 10 times 4 is 40. Adding those gives 340. Yep, that's the same answer. So, all methods lead to 340. I think that's solid.\n\nWait, just to make sure there's no trick here. The question says \"the product of 10 and 34.\" So, it's straightforward multiplication. There's no need to consider negative numbers or anything like that. The numbers are both positive, so the product should be positive. 10 times 34 is definitely positive. So, 340 is the correct answer.\n\nI don't see any errors in my reasoning. Breaking down 34 into 30 and 4, multiplying each by 10, and then adding gives 340. Alternatively, using the distributive property also gives the same result. So, I'm confident that 10 times 34 is 340.\n\n<|end_of_thought|>\n\n<|begin_of_solution|>\n\nThe product of 10 and 34 is calculated by multiplying the two numbers together. Breaking down 34 into 30 and 4, we get:\n\n\\[\n10 \\times 30 = 300 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 10 \\times 4 = 40\n\\]\n\nAdding these results together:\n\n\\[\n300 + 40 = 340\n\\]\n\nAlternatively, using the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n10 \\times (30 + 4) = 10 \\times 30 + 10 \\times 4 = 300 + 40 = 340\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm the product is \\boxed{340}.\n\n<|end_of_solution|>",
