      "index": 0,
      "text": "<|begin_of_thought|>\n\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 25 and 66. Hmm, let me think. Multiplying two numbers together. I remember that when you multiply a number by 10, you just add a zero to the end. But here, one of the numbers is 25, which is 25 times 10, right? So maybe I can break this down into smaller parts. Let me try that.\n\nFirst, 25 times 66. Let's see, 25 times 60 is 1500, and 25 times 6 is 150. Then, adding those two results together. 1500 plus 150 is 1650. Wait, is that right? Let me check. 25 times 60 is indeed 1500, and 25 times 6 is 150. So adding them gives 1500 + 150 = 1650. That seems correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method. Let's see, 25 times 66. Let's think of 66 as 60 + 6. So, 25 times (60 + 6) would be 25 times 60 plus 25 times 6. Calculating each part: 25 times 60 is 1500, and 25 times 6 is 150. Adding those gives 1500 + 150 = 1650. Same result. Okay, that's another way to do it.\n\nWait, maybe I can use the distributive property. Let's see, 25 times 66. Breaking it down into 25 times 60 plus 25 times 6. 25 times 60 is 1500, and 25 times 6 is 150. Adding those gives 1650. Yep, same answer.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the standard multiplication algorithm. Let's write 25 and 66 vertically. Multiply 25 by 60 first. 25 times 60 is 1500. Then, multiply 25 by 6, which is 150. Adding those two products together gives 1500 + 150 = 1650. That's the same answer again.\n\nHmm, all these methods lead to the same result. So, I can be confident that the product of 25 and 66 is 1650. Let me just verify once more to make sure I didn't make a mistake. 25 times 60 is 1500, 25 times 6 is 150. Adding them gives 1650. Yep, that's correct. I don't see any errors here. So, the answer should be 1650.\n\n<|end_of_thought|>\n\n<|begin_of_solution|>\n\nThe product of 25 and 66 is calculated as follows:\n\n1. **Break down 66 into 60 + 6**:  \n   \\( 25 \\times 66 = 25 \\times (60 + 6) = 25 \\times 60 + 25 \\times 6 \\).\n\n2. **Calculate each part**:  \n   - \\( 25 \\times 60 = 1500 \\)  \n   - \\( 25 \\times 6 = 150 \\)\n\n3. **Add the results**:  \n   \\( 1500 + 150 = 1650 \\).\n\nAlternatively, using the distributive property:  \n\\( 25 \\times 66 = 25 \\times (60 + 6) = 25 \\times 60 + 25 \\times 6 = 1500 + 150 = 1650 \\).\n\n**Final Answer**:  \n\\boxed{1650}\n\n<|end_of_solution|>",
