      "text": "<|begin_of_thought|>\n\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 97 and 34. Hmm, let me think. Multiplying two numbers can be tricky, but maybe I can break it down into smaller parts. Let me recall how to multiply two numbers with different digits. \n\nFirst, I remember that when multiplying two numbers, you can use the standard algorithm, which involves multiplying each digit of the first number by each digit of the second number, then adding those products together. But since these numbers are 97 and 34, which are both two-digit numbers, maybe I can use the distributive property here. \n\nAlternatively, I can use the fact that 97 is 100 - 3, and 34 is 34. So, maybe I can express 97 as 100 - 3 and then multiply that by 34. Let me try that. \n\nSo, (100 - 3) * 34. Let's distribute the 34 to both terms inside the parentheses. That would be 100*34 - 3*34. \n\nCalculating each part separately. 100*34. Well, 100*30 is 3000 and 100*4 is 400, so adding those together gives 3400. Then, 3*34. Let's compute that. 3*30 is 90 and 3*4 is 12, so 90+12=102. \n\nSo, putting it all together, 3400 - 102. Subtracting 102 from 3400. Let me do that. 3400 - 100 is 3300, then subtract 2 more, so 3300 - 2 = 3298. \n\nWait, let me check that again. 3400 minus 100 is 3300, then subtract 2 more is 3298. That seems right. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method. Let's see. 97*34. Let's try to break it down. 97*30 is 2910, and 97*4 is 388. Then add those two results together. 2910 + 388. Hmm, 2910 + 300 is 3210, then +88 is 3298. Same answer. \n\nSo, both methods give me 3298. Let me verify once more. 97*34. Let's compute 97*30 first. 97*30 is 2910. Then 97*4 is 388. Adding them together: 2910 + 388. Let's do 2910 + 300 = 3210, then +88 = 3298. Yep, that's correct. \n\nI think that's solid. So, the product of 97 and 34 is 3298. \n\n<|end_of_thought|>\n\n<|begin_of_solution|>\n\nTo find the product of \\(97\\) and \\(34\\), we can use the distributive property or break down the numbers into smaller parts. \n\n**Method 1: Distributive Property**\n\\[\n(100 - 3) \\times 34 = 100 \\times 34 - 3 \\times 34\n\\]\nCalculating each part:\n\\[\n100 \\times 34 = 3400 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 3 \\times 34 = 102\n\\]\nSubtracting these results:\n\\[\n3400 - 102 = 3298\n\\]\n\n**Method 2: Direct Multiplication**\n\\[\n97 \\times 34\n\\]\nBreaking down \\(97\\) into \\(100 - 3\\):\n\\[\n(100 - 3) \\times 34 = 100 \\times 34 - 3 \\times 34\n\\]\nCalculating each part:\n\\[\n100 \\times 34 = 3400 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 3 \\times 34 = 102\n\\]\nSubtracting these results:\n\\[\n3400 - 102 = 3298\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm the product is \\(\\boxed{3298}\\).\n\n<|end_of_solution|>",
