      "index": 0,
      "text": "<|begin_of_thought|>\n\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 36 and 22. Hmm, let me think. Multiplying two numbers together. I remember that when you multiply them, you can use the standard multiplication method, which is adding one number to itself as many times as the other number. But maybe there's a shortcut or a formula I can use here. Let me recall.\n\nWait, the formula for multiplying two numbers is (a \u00d7 b) = a \u00d7 (b + b + b + ... + b) or something like that. But maybe there's a simpler way. Let me try breaking it down. 36 times 22. Let's see, 36 times 20 is 720, and 36 times 2 is 72. Then adding those two results together: 720 + 72. Let me compute that. 720 + 70 is 790, and then +2 is 792. So the product should be 792. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the distributive property. For example, 36 \u00d7 22 = 36 \u00d7 (20 + 2) = 36 \u00d7 20 + 36 \u00d7 2. 36 \u00d7 20 is 720, and 36 \u00d7 2 is 72. Adding those gives 792 again. That seems consistent. \n\nWait, but let me check if there's another way. Maybe using the concept of multiples. 36 is 36, and 22 is 22. If I think of 36 as 3 tens and 6 ones, and 22 as 2 tens and 2 ones. Then, when you multiply them, you can think of 3 tens times 2 tens, which is 6 tens squared, so 600, and 6 ones times 2 ones, which is 12, so adding those together gives 612. But that's 612, which is different from 792. Hmm, so that method might not be correct here. \n\nAlternatively, maybe using the fact that 36 is 36, and 22 is 22. If I think of 36 as 36, and 22 as 22, then maybe I can use the formula for the product of two numbers where one is a multiple of the other. For example, if I have 36 \u00d7 22, and 22 is 10 times 2. So 36 \u00d7 22 = 36 \u00d7 (10 \u00d7 2) = (36 \u00d7 10) \u00d7 2 = 360 \u00d7 2 = 720. Then, 360 \u00d7 2 is 720, and 36 \u00d7 2 is 72. Adding those gives 792. So that's the same result. \n\nAlternatively, maybe using the formula for the product of two numbers where one is a multiple of the other. Let me see. If I have a \u00d7 b = a \u00d7 (b + b + b + ... + b) or something. Let me try that. 36 \u00d7 22 = 36 \u00d7 (22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 + 22 +