      "text": "<|begin_of_thought|>\n\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 68 and 47. Hmm, let me think about how to do this. I remember that multiplying two numbers can sometimes be tricky, but maybe there's a way to break it down into smaller parts to make it easier. Let me try that approach.\n\nFirst, I can use the distributive property of multiplication over addition. That means I can express 68 as 60 + 8 and 47 as 40 + 7. Then, multiplying these two groups together would give me (60 + 8)(40 + 7). Let me write that out:\n\n(60 + 8)(40 + 7) = 60*40 + 60*7 + 8*40 + 8*7\n\nNow, let's compute each of these terms separately. Starting with 60*40. Well, 60 times 40 is 2400. That's straightforward.\n\nNext, 60*7. Let me think. 60 times 7 is 420. Because 60*7 is 400 + 20 = 420.\n\nThen, 8*40. That's 320. Because 8*40 is 320.\n\nAnd finally, 8*7. That's 56. Because 8*7 is 56.\n\nNow, adding all these results together: 2400 + 420 + 320 + 56. Let me add them step by step.\n\nFirst, 2400 + 420 = 2820. Then, 2820 + 320 = 3140. Then, 3140 + 56 = 3196.\n\nWait, let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Let's add them again:\n\n2400 + 420 = 2820\n\n2820 + 320 = 3140\n\n3140 + 56 = 3196\n\nYes, that seems right. So the product of 68 and 47 is 3196. Let me just verify by multiplying them directly to be sure. 68*47. Let's do that:\n\n68*40 = 2720\n\n68*7 = 476\n\nAdding those together: 2720 + 476. Let's compute that. 2720 + 400 = 3120, then +76 = 3196. Yep, that matches. So both methods give the same result. That gives me more confidence that 3196 is the correct answer.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method, like the standard multiplication algorithm. Let's try that. 68*47. Let's write them vertically:\n\n  68\n\u00d7 47\n-----\n\nMultiply 68 by 7 first. 68*7. Let's do that. 60*7=420, 8*7=56. So 420+56=476. So the rightmost digit is 6.\n\nNow, multiply 68 by 40. 68*40. 60*40=2400, 8*40=320. So 2400+320=2720. So the middle digit is 2.\n\nNow, add the two products together: 476 + 2720. Let's add them step by step. 476 + 2000 = 2476, then +720 = 3196. Same result. So that's another way to confirm.\n\nTherefore, I can be confident that the product of 68 and 47 is indeed 3196. I don't see any mistakes in the calculations. Both methods lead to the same answer, so I think that's solid. I don't need to check further because the answer checks out.\n\n<|end_of_thought|>\n\n<|begin_of_solution|>\n\nTo find the product of \\(68 \\times 47\\), we can use the distributive property of multiplication over addition. Breaking down \\(68\\) as \\(60 + 8\\) and \\(47\\) as \\(40 + 7\\), we get:\n\n\\[\n(60 + 8)(40 + 7) = 60 \\times 40 + 60 \\times 7 + 8 \\times 40 + 8 \\times 7\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term separately:\n\n- \\(60 \\times 40 = 2400\\)\n- \\(60 \\times 7 = 420\\)\n- \\(8 \\times 40 = 320\\)\n- \\(8 \\times 7 = 56\\)\n\nAdding these results together:\n\n\\[\n2400 + 420 = 2820 \\\\\n2820 + 320 = 3140 \\\\\n3140 + 56 = 3196\n\\]\n\nVerification by direct multiplication confirms the result:\n\n\\[\n68 \\times 47 = 68 \\times (40 + 7) = 68 \\times 40 + 68 \\times 7 = 2720 + 476 = 3196\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of \\(68\\) and \\(47\\) is \\(\\boxed{3196}\\).\n\n<|end_of_solution|>",
