      "index": 0,
      "text": "<|begin_of_thought|>\n\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 90 and 47. Hmm, let me think. Multiplying two numbers together. I remember that when multiplying, you can break down one of the numbers into smaller parts to make the multiplication easier. Let me try that approach here.\n\nFirst, let's look at 90. I know that 90 is 9 times 10. So maybe I can use that to simplify the multiplication. Let me write that down: 90 \u00d7 47 = (9 \u00d7 10) \u00d7 47. That seems like a good start.\n\nNow, multiplying 9 by 10. Well, 9 times 10 is just 90. So that part is straightforward. So now the problem becomes 90 \u00d7 47 = 90 \u00d7 40 + 90 \u00d7 7. Let me verify that. Yes, because when you multiply a number by 10, you just add a zero to the end. So 90 \u00d7 40 is 900, and 90 \u00d7 7 is 630. Then adding those two results together: 900 + 630. Let me compute that. 900 + 600 is 1500, and then 1500 + 30 is 1530. So the total product should be 1530.\n\nWait, let me double-check my steps to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Starting with 90 \u00d7 47. Breaking it down into 90 \u00d7 40 + 90 \u00d7 7. 90 \u00d7 40 is indeed 3600, and 90 \u00d7 7 is 630. Adding those gives 3600 + 630. Let me add them step by step. 3600 + 600 is 4200, and 4200 + 30 is 4230. Yep, that's correct. So the product is 4230. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method to verify. Let's try the standard multiplication method. 90 \u00d7 47. Let's write it out:\n\n  90\n\u00d747\n-----\n  630  (90\u00d77)\n+3600  (90\u00d740)\n-----\n4230\n\nYes, that's the same result. So both methods give 4230. That seems solid. I don't think I made any errors here. The key was breaking down 90 into 9\u00d710, which allowed me to handle the multiplication more easily. Then, adding the results of 9\u00d740 and 9\u00d77. Both of those are straightforward multiplications, and adding them gives the total. \n\nAnother way to check is to use estimation. Let's see, 90 is close to 100, and 47 is close to 50. So 100\u00d750 is 5000. Since 90 is less than 100 and 47 is less than 50, the actual product should be less than 5000. 4230 is indeed less than 5000, so that checks out. \n\nI think that's thorough. All methods lead to the same answer, so I can be confident that 90\u00d747 equals 4230. No steps seem to be missing or incorrect. The multiplication of 90 and 47 is 4230.<|end_of_thought|>\n\n<|begin_of_solution|>\n\nTo find the product of \\(90 \\times 47\\):\n\n1. **Break down 90 into 90 and 10**:  \n   \\(90 \\times 47 = (90 \\times 10) \\times 47 = 900 \\times 47\\).\n\n2. **Multiply 900 by 47**:  \n   - \\(900 \\times 40 = 36000\\),  \n   - \\(900 \\times 7 = 6300\\),  \n   - Adding these results: \\(36000 + 6300 = 42300\\).\n\n3. **Verification using estimation**:  \n   - \\(90 \\approx 100\\) and \\(47 \\approx 50\\), so \\(100 \\times 50 = 5000\\), which is close to the actual product of 4230.\n\nBoth methods confirm the product is \\(\\boxed{4230}\\).\n\n<|end_of_solution|>",
