      "text": "<|begin_of_thought|>\n\nOkay, let's see. The question is asking for the product of 54 and 10. Hmm, product means multiplication, right? So I need to multiply 54 by 10. Let me think about how to do this. \n\nFirst, I remember that when multiplying a number by 10, you just add a zero to the end of the number. So, 54 times 10 should be 54 followed by a zero. Let me check that. 54 times 10 is 540. Yeah, that seems right. \n\nWait, maybe I should verify this another way to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Let's break it down. 54 times 10 is the same as 54 multiplied by 10. If I think of 10 as 10 ones, then 54 times 10 would be 54 times 10 ones. That would be 54 tens, which is 540. Yep, that matches. \n\nAlternatively, I can use the distributive property. 54 times 10 can be written as 50 times 10 plus 4 times 10. 50 times 10 is 500, and 4 times 10 is 40. Adding those together gives 500 + 40 = 540. That's another way to confirm the answer. \n\nI think all these methods lead to the same result, so I can be confident that the product of 54 and 10 is 540. Let me just recap to make sure I didn't skip any steps. The problem is straightforward: multiply 54 by 10. The key is recognizing that multiplying by 10 is equivalent to adding a zero to the end of the number. Since 54 has two digits, adding a zero gives 540. All checks confirm this is correct. \n\nNo, I don't see any errors here. The multiplication is straightforward, and the reasoning is solid. So, the final answer should be 540.\n\n<|end_of_thought|>\n\n<|begin_of_solution|>\n\nThe product of 54 and 10 is calculated by multiplying the two numbers. Since multiplying by 10 adds a zero to the end of the number, we have:\n\n\\[\n54 \\times 10 = 540\n\\]\n\nVerification using the distributive property confirms the result:\n\\[\n54 \\times 10 = 50 \\times 10 + 4 \\times 10 = 500 + 40 = 540\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm the answer is \\boxed{540}.\n\n<|end_of_solution|>",
