      "text": "<|begin_of_thought|>\n\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 15 and 34. Hmm, let me think. Multiplying two numbers together. Let me recall how to do this. I remember that when you multiply two numbers, you can break them down into smaller parts. Maybe I can use the distributive property here. \n\nThe distributive property says that a*(b + c) = a*b + a*c. So, if I can express 34 as 30 + 4, then I can multiply 15 by 30 and 15 by 4 separately, and then add those two results together. Let me try that.\n\nFirst, 15 times 30. Well, 15 times 30 is straightforward. 15 times 3 is 45, and then times 10 is 450. So, 15*30=450.\n\nNext, 15 times 4. That's a bit smaller. 15 times 4 is 60. So, 15*4=60.\n\nNow, adding those two results together: 450 + 60. Let me do that. 450 + 60 is 510. So, 15*34=510.\n\nWait, let me check if that's right. Maybe I can do it another way to verify. Let's see, 15*30 is 450, and 15*4 is 60. Adding them gives 510. That seems right. \n\nAlternatively, I can use another method. Let's see, 34 is 30 + 4. So, 15*(30 + 4) = 15*30 + 15*4. Which is 450 + 60 = 510. Yep, same answer. \n\nIs there a different way to approach this? Maybe using the standard multiplication algorithm. Let's try that. 15 and 34. \n\nFirst, write them vertically. \n\n  15\n\u00d7 34\n------\n\nMultiply 15 by 4 (the ones digit of 34). 15*4 is 60. Write 0 in the ones place and carry over 6.\n\nThen, multiply 15 by 30 (the tens digit of 34). 15*30 is 450. Add the carried over 6, so 450 + 6 = 456. Write this under the first row, shifted one place to the left.\n\nSo, putting it all together: 456. So, 15*34=456. \n\nHmm, that's another way to get the same answer. So, all methods lead to 456. \n\nWait, but let me check if 15*34 is indeed 456. Let me do the multiplication again step by step. \n\n15\n\u00d7 34\n-----\n15\n\u00d7 30 = 450\n15\n\u00d7 4 = 60\n-----\n450 + 60 = 510. Yes, that's correct. \n\nAlternatively, 15*30 is 450, 15*4 is 60, 450+60=510. \n\nAlternatively, 15