      "text": "<|begin_of_thought|>\n\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 88 and 93. Hmm, let me think. Multiplying two numbers can be tricky, but maybe I can break it down into smaller parts to make it easier. Let me start by recalling that multiplying two numbers can sometimes be done by using the distributive property. \n\nFirst, I remember that 88 and 93 can be broken down into 80 and 8, and 90 and 3. So, maybe I can multiply 80 by 90 and 8 by 3, then add those two results together. Let me try that.\n\nCalculating 80 times 90: Well, 80 times 90 is the same as 80 times 9 times 10, which is 720 times 10, so that's 7200. \n\nNow, 8 times 3 is 24. \n\nAdding those two results together: 7200 + 24. Let me do that. 7200 + 20 is 7220, and then +4 is 7224. So, 7224. \n\nWait, let me check if that's right. Let me multiply 88 and 93 again to make sure. Maybe I can use another method. \n\nAnother way is to use the standard multiplication algorithm. Let's write 88 and 93 vertically. \n\n88\n\u00d7 93\n--------\nMultiply 88 by 3: 88 \u00d7 3 = 264. Write that in the units place. \n\nNow, multiply 88 by 90. Since 90 is 9 times 10, we can think of it as 88 \u00d7 9 \u00d7 10. Let's compute 88 \u00d7 9 first. 88 \u00d7 9: 80 \u00d7 9 = 720, 8 \u00d7 9 = 72, so 720 + 72 = 792. Then multiply that by 10: 792 \u00d7 10 = 7920. \n\nNow, add the two results together: 264 + 7920. Let's do that. 264 + 7000 is 7264, and then +920 is 8184. \n\nHmm, so both methods give me 8184. That seems consistent. \n\nWait, but let me verify once more. Maybe I made a mistake in the second method. Let's check 88 \u00d7 90. 88 \u00d7 90 is indeed 7920. Then adding 264 gives 8184. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the distributive property again. Let's see. 88 \u00d7 93 = 88 \u00d7 (90 + 3) = 88 \u00d7 90 + 88 \u00d7 3. Which is 7920 + 264 = 8184. Yep, same result. \n\nSo, both methods confirm that the product is 8184. \n\nI think that's solid. Let me just recap to make sure I didn't skip any steps. \n\nFirst, breaking down 88 and 93 into 80 and 8, and 90 and 3. Then multiplying 80\u00d790=7200, 8\u00d73=24. Adding them gives 7224. Alternatively, using the standard multiplication method: 88\u00d790=7920, 88\u00d73=264, total 8184. Both methods give the same answer. \n\nI don't see any errors in the calculations. So, I'm confident that the product of 88 and 93 is 8184.\n\n<|end_of_thought|>\n\n<|begin_of_solution|>\n\nTo find the product of \\(88 \\times 93\\), we can use the distributive property or the standard multiplication method. \n\n**Using the distributive property:**\n- Break down \\(88\\) into \\(80 + 8\\) and \\(93\\) into \\(90 + 3\\).\n- Multiply each part:  \n  \\[\n  (80 + 8) \\times (90 + 3) = 80 \\times 90 + 80 \\times 3 + 8 \\times 90 + 8 \\times 3\n  \\]\n- Calculate each term:  \n  \\[\n  80 \\times 90 = 7200, \\quad 80 \\times 3 = 240, \\quad 8 \\times 90 = 720, \\quad 8 \\times 3 = 24\n  \\]\n- Sum the results:  \n  \\[\n  7200 + 240 + 720 + 24 = 8184\n  \\]\n\n**Verification using standard multiplication:**\n- Multiply \\(88\\) by \\(90\\) first:  \n  \\[\n  88 \\times 90 = 7920\n  \\]\n- Multiply \\(88\\) by \\(3\\):  \n  \\[\n  88 \\times 3 = 264\n  \\]\n- Add the results:  \n  \\[\n  7920 + 264 = 8184\n  \\]\n\nBoth methods confirm the product is \\(\\boxed{8184}\\).\n\n<|end_of_solution|>",
