en, expanding that: 20*80 + 20*8 + 7*80 + 7*8. Which is 1600 + 160 + 560 + 56. Adding those: 1600 + 160 is 1760, 560 + 56 is 616. So, 1760 + 616 = 2376. \n\nSame result. \n\nSo, all methods lead to 2376. Therefore, the product of 27 and 88 is 2376. \n\nI think that's solid. Let me just check once more. 27*80 is 2160, 27*8 is 216. 2160 + 216 = 2376. Yep, that's correct. \n\nAlternatively, 27*88. Let's compute 27*80 first. 27*80 is 2160. Then, 27*8 is 216. Adding them gives 2376. \n\nAlternatively, 27*88. Let's compute 27*80 first. 27*80 is 2160. Then, 27*8 is 216. Adding them gives 2376. \n\nYes, all methods confirm that the product is 2376. \n\nI don't see any mistakes here. The calculations check out. So, the answer should be 2376.\n\n<|end_of_thought|>\n\n<|begin_of_solution|>\n\nTo find the product of \\(27\\) and \\(88\\), we can use the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n27 \\times 88 = 27 \\times (80 + 8) = 27 \\times 80 + 27 \\times 8\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term separately:\n\n1. \\(27 \\times 80 = 2160\\)\n2. \\(27 \\times 8 = 216\\)\n\nAdding these results together:\n\n\\[\n2160 + 216 = 2376\n\\]\n\nVerification using another method (e.g., \\(27 \\times 80 = 2160\\), \\(27 \\times 8 = 216\\), and \\(2160 + 216 = 2376\\)) confirms the answer.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2376}\n\\]\n\n<|end_of_solution|>",
