      "text": "<|begin_of_thought|>\n\nOkay, let's see. The question is asking for the product of 12 and 31. Hmm, so I need to multiply these two numbers together. Let me think about how to do that. \n\nFirst, I remember that multiplication is just repeated addition. So, 12 times 31 means adding 12 to itself 31 times. That might be a bit tedious if I do it manually, but maybe there's a smarter way. Alternatively, I can use the distributive property of multiplication over addition. \n\nBreaking down 31 into 30 and 1, I can rewrite the multiplication as 12 times 30 plus 12 times 1. That would make it easier to compute each part separately. Let me check that. \n\n12 times 30 is straightforward. 12 times 30 is 360 because 10 times 30 is 300, and 2 times 30 is 60, so 300 + 60 = 360. \n\nThen, 12 times 1 is just 12. \n\nAdding those two results together: 360 + 12. Let me do that. 360 + 10 is 370, and then 370 + 2 is 372. \n\nWait, so the product of 12 and 31 is 372. Let me verify that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method. Let's see. 31 is 30 + 1, so 12 times 30 is 360, and 12 times 1 is 12. Adding them gives 360 + 12 = 372. Yep, that's the same answer. \n\nAnother way to check is to use division. If I divide 372 by 31, should I get 12? Let me try that. 31 times 12 is 372. Because 31 times 10 is 310, and 31 times 2 is 62, so 310 + 62 = 372. So that's correct. \n\nSo all methods lead to the same answer. Therefore, the product of 12 and 31 is 372. \n\nI think that's solid. Let me just recap the steps to make sure I didn't skip anything. \n\n1. Recognize that multiplication is repeated addition. \n2. Break down 31 into 30 + 1. \n3. Apply the distributive property: 12*(30 + 1) = 12*30 + 12*1. \n4. Calculate each part: 12*30 = 360, 12*1 = 12. \n5. Add the results: 360 + 12 = 372. \n6. Verify by division: 372 \u00f7 31 = 12. \n\nAll checks out. I don't see any errors here. So the answer should be 372.",
