      "text": "<|begin_of_thought|>\n\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 90 and 44. Hmm, let me think. Multiplying two numbers together. I remember that when multiplying, you can break down one of the numbers into smaller parts to make the multiplication easier. Let me try that approach here.\n\nFirst, I can think of 90 as 9 tens. So, 90 multiplied by 44 would be 9 tens multiplied by 44. That might be easier to handle. Let me write that out:\n\n90 \u00d7 44 = (9 \u00d7 44) \u00d7 10\n\nNow, I need to calculate 9 \u00d7 44 first. Let me do that. 9 times 40 is 360, and 9 times 4 is 36. Adding those together gives 360 + 36 = 396. So, 90 \u00d7 44 = 396. Then, since there's a 10 in the second part, I just need to add a zero to the end of 396. That would make 3960. \n\nWait, let me double-check that. Maybe I can do the multiplication another way to confirm. Let's see, 90 \u00d7 44. If I break it down, 90 \u00d7 40 is 3600, and 90 \u00d7 4 is 360. Adding those together gives 3600 + 360 = 3960. Yep, that matches. So, the product is 3960. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method. Let's see, 44 is 40 + 4. So, 90 \u00d7 (40 + 4) = 90 \u00d7 40 + 90 \u00d7 4. Calculating each part: 90 \u00d7 40 is 3600, and 90 \u00d7 4 is 360. Adding those gives 3600 + 360 = 3960. Same result. \n\nI think that's solid. So, the product of 90 and 44 is 3960. Let me just make sure there's no mistake in the calculation. 90 times 40 is 3600, 90 times 4 is 360, adding them gives 3960. Yep, that's correct. \n\nAlternatively, if I use the standard multiplication method, 90 \u00d7 44. Let's write it out:\n\n  90\n\u00d744\n-----\n  360  (90\u00d74)\n+3600 (90\u00d740)\n-----\n 3960\n\nYes, that's the same as before. So, all methods lead to the same answer. Therefore, I can be confident that the product is 3960.\n\n<|end_of_thought|>\n\n<|begin_of_solution|>\n\nThe product of 90 and 44 is calculated as follows:\n\n1. **Break down 90 into tens**:  \n   \\( 90 = 9 \\times 10 \\)\n\n2. **Multiply by 44**:  \n   \\( 9 \\times 44 \\)\n\n3. **Calculate \\( 9 \\times 44 \\)**:  \n   - \\( 9 \\times 40 = 360 \\)  \n   - \\( 9 \\times 4 = 36 \\)  \n   - \\( 360 + 36 = 396 \\)\n\n4. **Multiply by 10**:  \n   \\( 396 \\times 10 = 3960 \\)\n\nVerification using another method:  \n- \\( 44 = 40 + 4 \\)  \n- \\( 90 \\times (40 + 4) = 90 \\times 40 + 90 \\times 4 = 3600 + 360 = 3960 \\)\n\nBoth methods confirm the result. The final answer is \\(\\boxed{3960}\\).\n\n<|end_of_solution|>",
