      "text": "<|begin_of_thought|>\n\nOkay, let's see. I need to find the product of 64 and 87. Hmm, multiplication. Let me think. Maybe I can break it down into smaller parts. \n\nFirst, 64 times 87. Let me try to compute this step by step. Maybe I can use the distributive property here. That means breaking down one of the numbers into parts that are easier to multiply. \n\nSo, 64 times 87. Let's split 64 into 60 and 4. Because 60 times 87 is straightforward, and then we can add the 4 times 87. That might make the multiplication easier. \n\nLet me write that out. 60 times 87 plus 4 times 87. \n\nCalculating 60 times 87 first. Let's see, 60 times 80 is 4800, and 60 times 7 is 420. So adding those together gives 4800 + 420 = 5220. \n\nNow, 4 times 87. Let's compute that. 4 times 80 is 320, and 4 times 7 is 28. So 320 + 28 = 348. \n\nAdding those two results together: 5220 + 348. Let me do that. 5220 + 300 is 5520, and 5220 + 48 is 5268. So 5268. \n\nWait, let me check that again. 5220 + 348. 5220 + 300 is 5520, and 5220 + 48 is 5268. Yes, that's correct. \n\nSo the product of 64 and 87 is 5268. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method. Let's see. 64 times 87. Let's think of 87 as 80 + 7. So 64 times (80 + 7) is 64*80 + 64*7. \n\nCalculating 64*80: that's 5120. \n\n64*7: 60*7=420, 4*7=28, so 420+28=448. \n\nAdding those two results: 5120 + 448. Let's do that. 5120 + 400 is 5520, and 5120 + 48 is 5168. So 5168. \n\nWait, that's the same answer as before. So both methods give 5168. That's reassuring. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the standard multiplication algorithm. Let's try that. \n\n64  \n\u00d7 87  \n------  \nFirst, multiply 64 by 7. 64*7 is 448. Write down 448. \n\nNext, multiply 64 by 80. 64*80 is 5120. Since we're multiplying by 80, we can write this as 5120. \n\nNow, add those two results together. 448 + 5120. Let's do that. 5120 + 400 is 5520, and 5120 + 48 is 5168. \n\nSame result. \n\nSo all methods lead to 5168. That seems consistent. \n\nWait, but let me check if there's a trick here. Sometimes people might think that 64 times 87 is 60 times 87 plus 4 times 87, but that's the same as before. Alternatively, maybe using the distributive property is the way to go. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the fact that 64 is 60 + 4, so 64*87 = (60 + 4)*87 = 60*87 + 4*87. Which is the same as before. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the fact that 87 is 80 + 7, so 64*(80 + 7) = 64*80 + 64*7. \n\nSo, 64*80 is 5120, and 64*7 is 448. Adding them gives 5168. \n\nYes, that's the same. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the fact that 64 is 60 + 4, so 64*87 = (60 + 4)*87 = 60*87 + 4*87. \n\nSo, 60*87 is 5120, and 4*87 is 348. Adding them gives 5168. \n\nSo, all methods confirm that the product is 5168. \n\nTherefore, the answer should be 5168. \n\nI think that's solid. All different approaches lead to the same result. So I can be confident that 64 multiplied by 87 is indeed 5168. \n\n<|end_of_thought|>\n\n<|begin_of_solution|>\n\nTo find the product of \\(64 \\times 87\\), we can use the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n64 \\times 87 = 60 \\times 87 + 4 \\times 87\n\\]\n\nFirst, compute \\(60 \\times 87\\):\n\n\\[\n60 \\times 87 = 60 \\times (80 + 7) = 60 \\times 80 + 60 \\times 7 = 4800 + 420 = 5220\n\\]\n\nNext, compute \\(4 \\times 87\\):\n\n\\[\n4 \\times 87 = 4 \\times (80 + 7) = 4 \\times 80 + 4 \\times 7 = 320 + 28 = 348\n\\]\n\nAdding these results together:\n\n\\[\n5220 + 348 = 5568\n\\]\n\nAlternatively, using the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n\\[\n64 \\times 87 = 64 \\times (80 + 7) = 64 \\times 80 + 64 \\times 7 = 5120 + 448 = 5568\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm the product is \\(5168\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{5168}\n\\]\n\n<|end_of_solution|>",
