      "text": "<|begin_of_thought|>\n\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 97 and 60. Hmm, let me think. Multiplying two numbers together. I remember that when multiplying, you can break down one of the numbers into smaller parts to make the multiplication easier. Maybe I can use the distributive property here. Let me try that.\n\nFirst, 97 can be broken down into 100 minus 3. So, 97 is 100 - 3. Then, multiplying that by 60 would be (100 - 3) * 60. Let me compute that step by step. \n\nFirst, 100 * 60 is straightforward. 100 times 60 is 6,000. Then, 3 * 60 is 180. So, subtracting those two results: 6,000 - 180. Let me do that subtraction. 6,000 minus 100 is 5,900, and then minus 80 is 5,820. So, 6,000 - 180 equals 5,820. \n\nWait, let me check that again. 100*60 is 6,000. Then, 3*60 is 180. So, 6,000 - 180 is indeed 5,820. So, the product of 97 and 60 is 5,820. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can do it another way. Let's see. 97*60. If I think of 60 as 6*10, then 97*6*10. Multiplying 97*6 first. Let's compute 97*6. Hmm, 97*6. 97*6 is 582. Then, 582*10 is 5,820. Same result. So, that's another way to get the answer. \n\nI think both methods confirm that the product is 5,820. Let me just verify once more. 97*60. 97*6 is 582, and 582*10 is 5,820. Yep, that's correct. \n\nIs there any chance I made a mistake in the first method? Let me check 100*60. 100*60 is 6,000. 3*60 is 180. 6,000 - 180 is 5,820. That seems right. \n\nAlternatively, if I use 97*60, maybe I can break it down differently. Let's see. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*60. 97*