      "text": "<|begin_of_thought|>\n\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 40 and 52. Hmm, let me think. Multiplying two numbers together. Let me recall how to do this. I remember that when multiplying, you can break down the numbers into smaller parts to make the multiplication easier. Maybe I can use the distributive property here. \n\nThe distributive property says that a*(b + c) = a*b + a*c. So, if I can express 52 as 50 + 2, then multiplying 40 by each part and adding them together should give me the same result. Let me try that.\n\nFirst, 40 times 50. Well, 40 times 50 is straightforward. 40 times 50 is 2000. Then, 40 times 2 is 80. So, adding those two results together: 2000 + 80. Let me calculate that. 2000 plus 80 is 2080. So, 40 times 52 is 2080. \n\nWait, let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake. 40 times 50 is definitely 2000. 40 times 2 is 80. Adding them gives 2080. That seems right. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method. Let's see. 52 can be written as 50 + 2. So, 40 times (50 + 2) is 40*50 + 40*2. As before, that's 2000 + 80 = 2080. Same answer. \n\nIs there another way to approach this? Maybe using the standard multiplication algorithm. Let's try that. 40 and 52. Aligning the numbers by their place values:\n\n  40\n\u00d7 52\n-----\n\nMultiply 40 by 2: 80. Write that down, aligning with the ones place. Then multiply 40 by 50, which is 2000. Now, add those two results together: 80 + 2000. That's 2080. Yep, same answer. \n\nI think that's solid. Both methods give me 2080. Let me just verify once more. 40 times 50 is 2000, 40 times 2 is 80. 2000 + 80 is indeed 2080. \n\nWait, but maybe I can check with another number. Let's take 40 times 50. 40 times 50 is 2000. Then 40 times 2 is 80. Adding them gives 2080. That's correct. \n\nAlternatively, if I take 40 times 50, which is 2000, and then add 40 times 2, which is 80. 2000 + 80 is 2080. Yep, that's the same. \n\nI don't see any mistakes here. Both methods lead to the same result. So, I'm confident that the product of 40 and 52 is 2080. \n\n<|end_of_thought|>\n\n<|begin_of_solution|>\n\nThe product of 40 and 52 can be calculated using the distributive property:  \n\\[\n40 \\times 52 = 40 \\times (50 + 2) = 40 \\times 50 + 40 \\times 2 = 2000 + 80 = 2080.\n\\]  \nAlternatively, using the standard multiplication algorithm:  \n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c@{}c@{}c}\n  & 4 & 0 \\\\\n\\times & 5 & 2 \\\\\n\\hline\n  & 8 & 0 \\\\\n+ & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\\n\\hline\n2 & 0 & 8 & 0 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]  \nBoth methods confirm the result is \\boxed{2080}.\n\n<|end_of_solution|>",
