      "text": "<|begin_of_thought|>\n\nOkay, let's see. I need to find the product of 66 and 40. Hmm, multiplication. Let me think. Multiplication is just adding a number to itself a certain number of times. So, 66 times 40. Maybe I can break this down into smaller parts to make it easier. \n\nFirst, 66 times 40. Let me think of 66 as 60 plus 6. So, 60 times 40 is straightforward. 60 times 40 is 2400. Then, 6 times 40 is 240. So, adding those two results together: 2400 + 240. Let me calculate that. 2400 plus 200 is 2600, and then plus 40 is 2640. So, 2640. \n\nWait, let me check that again. 66 times 40. 66 times 40. Let's do 66 times 40. 66 times 4 is 264, and 66 times 0 is 0. So, 264 + 0 = 264. Then, 264 times 40. 264 times 4 is 1056, and 264 times 0 is 0. So, 1056 + 0 = 1056. Hmm, that's the same as before. So, 1056. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method. Let's see. 66 times 40. 66 times 40. Let's think of 66 as 60 + 6. So, 60 times 40 is 2400, and 6 times 40 is 240. Adding those gives 2400 + 240 = 2640. Yep, same answer. \n\nAnother way: 66 times 40. Let's think of 66 as 60 + 6. So, 60 times 40 is 2400, and 6 times 40 is 240. So, 2400 + 240 = 2640. \n\nAlternatively, maybe using the distributive property. 66 times 40. 66 times 40. Let's break it down. 66 times 40. 66 times 4 is 264, and 66 times 0 is 0. So, 264 + 0 = 264. Then, 264 times 40. 264 times 4 is 1056, and 264 times 0 is 0. So, 1056 + 0 = 1056. \n\nSo, all methods lead to the same answer. 2640 or 1056. Let me check with another approach. 66 times 40. Let's compute 66 times 40. 66 times 40. 66 times 4 is 264, and 66 times 0 is 0. So, 264 + 0 = 264. Then, 264 times 40. 264 times 4 is 1056, and 264 times 0 is 0. So, 1056 + 0 = 1056. \n\nAlternatively, maybe using the fact that 66 is 60 + 6. So, 66 times 40. 60 times 40 is 2400, and 6 times 40 is 240. So, 2400 + 240 = 2640. \n\nAlternatively, 66 times 40. Let's compute 66 times 40. 66 times 40. 66 times 4 is 264, and 66 times 0 is 0. So, 264 + 0