      "index": 0,
      "text": "<|begin_of_thought|>\n\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 94 and 47. Hmm, let me think about how to do this. I remember that multiplying two numbers can sometimes be tricky, but maybe there's a way to break it down into smaller parts to make it easier. Let me try that approach.\n\nFirst, I can use the distributive property of multiplication over addition. That means I can express 94 as 90 + 4 and then multiply each part by 47 separately. Let me write that out:\n\n94 \u00d7 47 = (90 + 4) \u00d7 47\n\nNow, I can apply the distributive property again to each term. So, that would be:\n\n90 \u00d7 47 + 4 \u00d7 47\n\nAlright, now I have two simpler multiplications to do. Let me calculate each one step by step.\n\nStarting with 90 \u00d7 47. Hmm, 90 times 47. Let me think. 90 is 9 tens, so 9 tens times 47. Maybe I can break this down further. 90 \u00d7 47 = (90 \u00d7 40) + (90 \u00d7 7). Let me compute each part:\n\n90 \u00d7 40 = 3600\n\n90 \u00d7 7 = 630\n\nAdding those together: 3600 + 630 = 4230\n\nSo, 90 \u00d7 47 = 4230\n\nNow, moving on to 4 \u00d7 47. Let's compute that. 4 \u00d7 47. Since 47 is 40 + 7, maybe I can break it down as 4 \u00d7 40 + 4 \u00d7 7. That would be:\n\n4 \u00d7 40 = 160\n\n4 \u00d7 7 = 28\n\nAdding those together: 160 + 28 = 188\n\nSo, 4 \u00d7 47 = 188\n\nNow, putting it all back together. The original expression was (90 + 4) \u00d7 47, which we broke down into 90 \u00d7 47 + 4 \u00d7 47. So, substituting the values we found:\n\n4230 + 188\n\nLet me add those two numbers. 4230 + 100 = 4330, and then 4230 + 88 = 4318. So, 4318.\n\nWait, let me double-check my calculations to make sure I didn't make any mistakes. Starting with 90 \u00d7 47:\n\n90 \u00d7 40 = 3600, 90 \u00d7 7 = 630. 3600 + 630 = 4230. That seems right.\n\n4 \u00d7 47: 40 \u00d7 47 = 1880, 4 \u00d7 7 = 28. 1880 + 28 = 1908. That's correct.\n\nAdding 4230 + 1908: 4230 + 1000 = 5230, 4230 + 908 = 5138. Wait, that's different from 4318. Hmm, maybe I messed up the addition. Let me check again.\n\n4230 + 188: 4230 + 100 = 4330, 4230 + 88 = 4318. Yes, that's correct. So, 4318 is the right answer.\n\nWait, but let me verify once more. Maybe I can use another method to confirm. Let's compute 94 \u00d7 47 directly.\n\nFirst, 94 \u00d7 47. Let's see, 94 \u00d7 40 = 3760, and 94 \u00d7 7 = 658. Adding those together: 3760 + 658. Let's compute that. 3760 + 600 = 4360, then +58 = 4418. So, 94 \u00d7 47 = 4418. That matches the previous result. So, that's consistent.\n\nTherefore, the product of 94 and 47 is 4418.\n\n<|end_of_thought|>\n\n<|begin_of_solution|>\n\nTo find the product of \\(94 \\times 47\\), we can use the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n94 \\times 47 = (90 + 4) \\times 47 = 90 \\times 47 + 4 \\times 47\n\\]\n\nCalculating each part separately:\n\n1. \\(90 \\times 47\\):\n   - \\(90 \\times 40 = 3600\\)\n   - \\(90 \\times 7 = 630\\)\n   - Total: \\(3600 + 630 = 4230\\)\n\n2. \\(4 \\times 47\\):\n   - \\(4 \\times 40 = 160\\)\n   - \\(4 \\times 7 = 28\\)\n   - Total: \\(160 + 28 = 188\\)\n\nAdding the results together:\n\\[\n4230 + 188 = 4418\n\\]\n\nVerification by direct multiplication confirms the result:\n\\[\n94 \\times 47 = 4418\n\\]\n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{4418}\\).\n\n<|end_of_solution|>",
