      "text": "<|begin_of_thought|>\n\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 81 and 61. Hmm, let me think. Multiplying two numbers can be tricky, but maybe I can break it down into smaller parts. Let me recall how to multiply two numbers with different digits. \n\nFirst, I remember that when multiplying two numbers, you can use the standard algorithm, which involves multiplying each digit of the first number by each digit of the second number, then adding those products together. But since both numbers are two-digit numbers, maybe there's a shortcut here. \n\nAlternatively, I can use the distributive property. For example, 81 times 61 can be written as (80 + 1) times (60 + 1). Then, applying the distributive property, that would be 80*60 + 80*1 + 1*60 + 1*1. Let me compute each part step by step.\n\nFirst, 80 times 60. Well, 80 times 60 is 4800. That's straightforward. Then, 80 times 1 is 80. Then, 1 times 60 is 60. And finally, 1 times 1 is 1. \n\nAdding those up: 4800 + 80 + 60 + 1. Let me add them in order. 4800 + 80 is 4880. Then, 4880 + 60 is 4940. Then, 4940 + 1 is 4941. \n\nWait, let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Let's add them step by step:\n\n4800 + 80 = 4880\n\n4880 + 60 = 4940\n\n4940 + 1 = 4941\n\nYes, that seems right. So the product of 81 and 61 is 4941. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method. Let's see. 81 times 61. Let's think of 81 as 80 + 1 and 61 as 60 + 1. So, 80*60 + 80*1 + 1*60 + 1*1. Which is 4800 + 80 + 60 + 1. Adding those gives 4941. \n\nHmm, same result. So that confirms it. \n\nWait, but maybe there's a way to compute this without breaking it down. Let's try another approach. Let's compute 81*60 first, which is 4860. Then, 81*1 is 81. So total is 4860 + 81. Let's compute that. 4860 + 80 is 4940, then +1 is 4941. Same answer. \n\nSo all methods lead to the same result. Therefore, the product of 81 and 61 is 4941. \n\nI think that's solid. Let me just check once more. 81 times 60 is 4860, plus 81 is 4941. Yep, that's correct. \n\n<|end_of_thought|>\n\n<|begin_of_solution|>\n\nThe product of 81 and 61 is calculated as follows:\n\n\\[\n81 \\times 61 = 80 \\times 60 + 80 \\times 1 + 1 \\times 60 + 1 \\times 1\n\\]\n\nBreaking down each term:\n\n- \\(80 \\times 60 = 4800\\)\n- \\(80 \\times 1 = 80\\)\n- \\(1 \\times 60 = 60\\)\n- \\(1 \\times 1 = 1\\)\n\nAdding these results together:\n\n\\[\n4800 + 80 + 60 + 1 = 4941\n\\]\n\nVerification using another method:\n\n- \\(81 \\times 60 = 4860\\)\n- \\(81 \\times 1 = 81\\)\n- Total: \\(4860 + 81 = 4941\\)\n\nBoth methods confirm the product is \\(\\boxed{4941}\\).\n\n<|end_of_solution|>",
