      "text": "<|begin_of_thought|>\n\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 50 and 84. Hmm, let me think. Multiplying two numbers together. Let me recall how to do this. I remember that when multiplying, you can break down one of the numbers into smaller parts to make the multiplication easier. Maybe I can use the distributive property here. \n\nLet me write down the numbers: 50 and 84. If I break down 50 into 50 and 10, that might help. Because 50 times 10 is straightforward. Then, 50 times 84 would be 50 times 10 plus 50 times 84. Let me calculate each part separately. \n\nFirst, 50 times 10 is 500. Then, 50 times 84. Hmm, 50 times 80 is 4000, and 50 times 4 is 200. So adding those together, 4000 + 200 is 4200. So 50 times 84 is 4200. \n\nWait, let me check that again. 50 times 80 is 4000, and 50 times 4 is 200. So 4000 + 200 is indeed 4200. That seems right. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method. Let's see. 84 times 50. Well, 84 times 50 is the same as 84 times 5 times 10, which is 420 times 10, so 4200. That also gives me 4200. \n\nSo both methods give me the same result. Let me verify once more. 50 times 84. Let's do it step by step. 50 times 80 is 4000, and 50 times 4 is 200. Adding them together: 4000 + 200 = 4200. Yep, that's correct. \n\nI don't think I made any mistakes here. Both methods lead to the same answer. So the product of 50 and 84 is 4200. \n\n<|end_of_thought|>\n\n<|begin_of_solution|>\n\nThe product of 50 and 84 is calculated as follows:\n\n1. **Break down 50 into 50 and 10**:  \n   \\( 50 \\times 84 = 50 \\times 10 + 50 \\times 84 \\).\n\n2. **Calculate each part**:  \n   - \\( 50 \\times 10 = 500 \\).  \n   - \\( 50 \\times 84 \\):  \n     - \\( 50 \\times 80 = 4000 \\)  \n     - \\( 50 \\times 4 = 200 \\)  \n     - \\( 4000 + 200 = 4200 \\).\n\n3. **Combine the results**:  \n   \\( 50 \\times 84 = 4200 \\).\n\n**Verification**:  \n- Using distributive property: \\( 50 \\times 80 + 50 \\times 4 = 4000 + 200 = 4200 \\).  \n- Direct multiplication: \\( 50 \\times 84 = 4200 \\).\n\nBoth methods confirm the product is \\(\\boxed{4200}\\).\n\n<|end_of_solution|>",
