      "index": 0,
      "text": "<|begin_of_thought|>\n\nOkay, let's see. I need to find the product of 77 and 38. Hmm, multiplication. Let me think. Maybe I can break it down into smaller parts. \n\nFirst, I remember that multiplying two numbers can sometimes be easier if I use the distributive property. So, 77 times 38 is the same as (70 + 7) times (30 + 8). That might make it simpler. Let me write that out:\n\n(70 + 7) * (30 + 8) = 70*30 + 70*8 + 7*30 + 7*8\n\nNow, let's calculate each part separately. \n\nFirst, 70 times 30. Well, 70 times 30 is 2100. That's straightforward. \n\nNext, 70 times 8. Let me do that. 70 times 8 is 560. \n\nThen, 7 times 30. That's 210. \n\nAnd 7 times 8. That's 56. \n\nNow, adding all those up: 2100 + 560 + 210 + 56. Let me add them step by step. \n\nStarting with 2100 + 560. That's 2660. Then, 2660 + 210. That's 2870. Then, 2870 + 56. Let's see, 2870 + 50 is 2920, and then +6 is 2926. \n\nWait, let me check that again. 2870 + 50 is 2920, then +6 is 2926. Yes, that's correct. \n\nSo, the total product is 2926. \n\nBut let me verify this another way to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Maybe using another method. Let's try the standard multiplication method. \n\n77 times 38. Let's write it as 77*30 + 77*8. \n\n77*30 is 2310. \n\n77*8 is 616. \n\nAdding those together: 2310 + 616. Let's do that. 2310 + 600 is 2910, then +16 is 2926. \n\nSame result. So that confirms it. \n\nAlternatively, maybe using the standard algorithm. Let's write 77 and 38 vertically. \n\n  77\n\u00d7 38\n-----\nMultiply 77 by 8 first. 77*8 is 616. \n\nThen, multiply 77 by 30, which is 2310. \n\nNow, add those two results: 616 + 2310. Let's do that. \n\n616 + 2000 is 2616, then +310 is 2926. \n\nYep, same answer. \n\nSo, all methods lead to 2926. I think that's solid. \n\nJust to recap, breaking down 77 into 70 + 7 and 38 into 30 + 8, then multiplying each part and adding them up. Alternatively, using the distributive property, which is essentially the same. \n\nI don't see any errors in the calculations. The steps check out. So, the product of 77 and 38 is 2926. \n\n<|end_of_thought|>\n\n<|begin_of_solution|>\n\nTo find the product of \\(77 \\times 38\\), we can use the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n77 \\times 38 = (70 + 7) \\times (30 + 8)\n\\]\n\nExpanding this using the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n70 \\times 30 + 70 \\times 8 + 7 \\times 30 + 7 \\times 8\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n- \\(70 \\times 30 = 2100\\)\n- \\(70 \\times 8 = 560\\)\n- \\(7 \\times 30 = 210\\)\n- \\(7 \\times 8 = 56\\)\n\nAdding these results together:\n\n\\[\n2100 + 560 = 2660 \\\\\n2660 + 210 = 2870 \\\\\n2870 + 56 = 2926\n\\]\n\nVerification using another method (standard multiplication):\n\n\\[\n77 \\times 38 = 77 \\times (30 + 8) = 77 \\times 30 + 77 \\times 8 = 2310 + 616 = 2926\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm the product is \\(\\boxed{2926}\\).\n\n<|end_of_solution|>",
