      "text": "<|begin_of_thought|>\n\nOkay, let's see. I need to find the product of 14 and 58. Hmm, multiplication. Let me think. Multiplication is like adding the same number multiple times. So, 14 times 58. Maybe I can break it down into smaller parts to make it easier. \n\nFirst, I remember that 58 is a two-digit number. Maybe I can split it into 50 and 8. So, 14 times 50 is straightforward. 14 times 50 is 700 because 10 times 50 is 500, and 4 times 50 is 200, so 500 + 200 = 700. \n\nNow, 14 times 8. Let me think. 14 times 8. Well, 10 times 8 is 80, and 4 times 8 is 32. So, 80 + 32 is 112. \n\nSo, adding those two results together: 700 + 112. Let me do that. 700 + 100 is 800, and 112 is 112. So, 800 + 112. Hmm, 800 + 100 is 900, and 112 is 900 + 12. So, 900 + 12 is 912. \n\nWait, let me check that again. 700 + 112. 700 + 100 is 800, and 112 is 112. So, 800 + 112 is 912. That seems right. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method. Let's see. 14 times 50 is 700, and 14 times 8 is 112. So, 700 + 112. Let's compute that. 700 + 100 is 800, and 112 is 800 + 32. So, 800 + 32 is 832. \n\nHmm, both methods give me 832. Let me verify once more. 14 times 50 is 700, and 14 times 8 is 112. Adding them: 700 + 112. Let's do 700 + 100 = 800, then 800 + 12 = 812. Yep, that's 812. \n\nWait, but 14 times 58 is 812. Let me check if that's correct. Maybe I can use another approach. Let's see. 58 times 14. Let's compute that. \n\n58 times 10 is 580. 58 times 4 is 232. So, 580 + 232. Let's add those. 580 + 200 is 780, and 232 is 780 + 32. So, 780 + 32 is 812. \n\nYes, that's the same result. So, 58 times 14 is 812. Therefore, 14 times 58 is 812. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the distributive property. 14 times 58 is 14 times (50 + 8). So, 14 times 50 is 700, and 14 times 8 is 112. Adding them gives 700 + 112 = 812. \n\nSo, all methods lead to the same answer. Therefore, the product of 14 and 58 is 812. \n\nWait, just to make sure I didn't make any mistakes in the calculations. Let's check 14 times 50. 14 times 50 is 700. Correct. 14 times 8 is 112. Correct. 700 + 112 is 812. Correct. \n\nAlternatively, 58 times 14. 58 times 10 is 580. 58 times 4 is 232. 580 + 232. 580 + 200 is 780, 232 is 780 + 32. 780 + 32 is 812. Correct. \n\nYes, all steps check out. So, the answer should be 812. \n\nI think that's thorough. I considered different methods, verified each step, and confirmed the result. So, I'm confident that 14 times 58 is 812.\n\n<|end_of_thought|>\n\n<|begin_of_solution|>\n\nTo find the product of \\(14\\) and \\(58\\), we can use the distributive property or break down the numbers into smaller parts. \n\n**Method 1: Distributive Property**\n\\[\n14 \\times 58 = 14 \\times (50 + 8) = 14 \\times 50 + 14 \\times 8\n\\]\nCalculating each part:\n\\[\n14 \\times 50 = 700 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 14 \\times 8 = 112\n\\]\nAdding these results:\n\\[\n700 + 112 = 812\n\\]\n\n**Method 2: Breaking Down 58**\n\\[\n58 = 50 + 8\n\\]\n\\[\n14 \\times 58 = 14 \\times (50 + 8) = 14 \\times 50 + 14 \\times 8\n\\]\nCalculating each part:\n\\[\n14 \\times 50 = 700 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 14 \\times 8 = 112\n\\]\nAdding these results:\n\\[\n700 + 112 = 812\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm the product is \\(\\boxed{812}\\).\n\n<|end_of_solution|>",
