      "text": "<|begin_of_thought|>\n\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 50 and 13. Hmm, let me think. Multiplying two numbers together. Let me recall how to do this. \n\nFirst, I remember that when multiplying a two-digit number by a single-digit number, you can break down the multiplication into simpler parts. Maybe I can use the distributive property here. Let me try that. \n\n50 multiplied by 13. If I split 50 into 50 and 10, then 50 times 13 would be (50 + 10) times 13. That's easier because I can multiply each part separately. \n\nSo, 50 times 13 is the same as (50 * 10) + (50 * 3). Let me compute each part. \n\n50 times 10 is straightforward: 50 times 10 is 500. \n\n50 times 3. Hmm, 50 times 3. Let me think. 50 times 3 is 150. \n\nSo adding those two results together: 500 + 150. That's 650. \n\nWait, let me check that again. 50 times 10 is 500, and 50 times 3 is 150. 500 + 150 is indeed 650. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can do it another way. Let's see. 50 times 13. Let's think of 13 as 10 + 3. So, 50 times (10 + 3) is 50*10 + 50*3. Which is 500 + 150 = 650. Same answer. \n\nIs there a quicker way? Maybe using the standard multiplication algorithm. Let's try that. \n\n50  \n\u00d7 13  \n------  \n500 (50 times 10)  \n+ 500 (50 times 3)  \n------  \n650  \nYes, that's the same as before. \n\nAlternatively, if I use the standard algorithm, I can write 50 and 13, align them, and multiply each digit. \n\nFirst, multiply 50 by 3: 50 times 3 is 150. Then, multiply 50 by 10, which is 500. Then add those two results: 150 + 500 = 650. \n\nAll methods lead to the same answer. So, the product of 50 and 13 is 650. \n\nWait, let me just verify once more. 50 times 10 is 500, 50 times 3 is 150. 500 + 150 is 650. Yep, that's correct. \n\nI don't think I made any mistakes here. The key was breaking down the multiplication into simpler parts using the distributive property. That's a good strategy when dealing with larger numbers. It makes the multiplication easier by breaking it into smaller, more manageable steps. \n\nSo, the final answer should be 650. I can't see any errors in my calculations. All methods confirm that 50 times 13 is indeed 650. \n\n<|end_of_thought|>\n\n<|begin_of_solution|>\n\nTo find the product of \\(50\\) and \\(13\\), we can use the distributive property by breaking down \\(50\\) into \\(50 + 10\\) and \\(13\\) into \\(10 + 3\\). This gives:\n\n\\[\n50 \\times 13 = (50 + 10) \\times 13 = 50 \\times 10 + 50 \\times 3\n\\]\n\nCalculating each part separately:\n\n\\[\n50 \\times 10 = 500 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 50 \\times 3 = 150\n\\]\n\nAdding these results together:\n\n\\[\n500 + 150 = 650\n\\]\n\nAlternatively, using the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n  50 \\\\\n\\times 13 \\\\\n\\hline\n  150 \\quad \\text{(50 times 3)} \\\\\n+500 \\quad \\text{(50 times 10)} \\\\\n\\hline\n 650 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm the product is \\(650\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{650}\n\\]\n\n<|end_of_solution|>",
