      "text": "<|begin_of_thought|>\n\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 59 and 65. Hmm, let me think. Multiplying two numbers can be tricky, but maybe I can break it down into smaller parts. Let me recall how to multiply two numbers with different digits. \n\nFirst, I remember that when multiplying two numbers, you can use the standard algorithm, which involves multiplying each digit of the first number by each digit of the second number, then adding those products together. But since these numbers are 59 and 65, which are both two-digit numbers, maybe I can simplify the multiplication by breaking them down into tens and ones. \n\nLet me try that. 59 can be thought of as 50 + 9, and 65 can be thought of as 60 + 5. So, multiplying these two numbers would be (50 + 9)(60 + 5). Then, using the distributive property, I can multiply each term in the first parentheses by each term in the second parentheses. \n\nSo, (50 * 60) + (50 * 5) + (9 * 60) + (9 * 5). Let me compute each of these parts step by step. \n\nFirst, 50 multiplied by 60. Well, 50 times 60 is 3000. That's straightforward. \n\nNext, 50 multiplied by 5. That's 250. \n\nThen, 9 multiplied by 60. Let me calculate that. 9 times 60 is 540. \n\nAnd finally, 9 multiplied by 5. That's 45. \n\nNow, adding all these results together: 3000 + 250 + 540 + 45. Let me add them step by step. \n\nStarting with 3000 + 250. That's 3250. Then, 3250 + 540. Let's see, 3250 + 500 is 3750, and then +40 is 3790. Then, 3790 + 45. That's 3835. \n\nWait, let me check that again. 3000 + 250 is 3250. Then 3250 + 540 is 3790. Then 3790 + 45 is 3835. Yes, that seems right. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method to verify. Let's see, 59 times 65. Let's try another approach. \n\nAnother way to multiply two numbers is to use the formula (a + b)(c + d) = ac + ad + bc + bd. So, in this case, a is 50, b is 9, c is 60, and d is 5. \n\nSo, ac is 50*60 = 3000. ad is 50*5 = 250. bc is 9*60 = 540. bd is 9*5 = 45. \n\nAdding those up: 3000 + 250 = 3250; 3250 + 540 = 3790; 3790 + 45 = 3835. Same result. \n\nSo, both methods give me 3835. That seems consistent. \n\nWait, but let me just double-check the multiplication steps again to make sure I didn't make any mistakes. \n\nFirst, 59 times 65. Let's do it step by step. \n\n59 * 60: 50*60=3000, 9*60=540. So 3000+540=3540. \n\n59 * 5: 59*5=295. \n\nAdding 3540 + 295. Let's compute that. 3540 + 200 = 3740; 3740 + 95 = 3835. Yep, that's correct. \n\nAlternatively, 59*65. Let's compute 59*60=3540, 59*5=295. 3540+295=3835. \n\nSo, both methods confirm the answer is 3835. \n\nI think that's solid. So, the product of 59 and 65 is 3835. \n\n<|end_of_thought|>\n\n<|begin_of_solution|>\n\nTo find the product of \\(59\\) and \\(65\\), we can use the distributive property by breaking down each number into tens and ones:\n\n\\[\n59 \\times 65 = (50 + 9)(60 + 5)\n\\]\n\nExpanding this using the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n= 50 \\times 60 + 50 \\times 5 + 9 \\times 60 + 9 \\times 5\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n\\[\n= 3000 + 250 + 540 + 45\n\\]\n\nAdding these results step by step:\n\n\\[\n3000 + 250 = 3250\n\\]\n\\[\n3250 + 540 = 3790\n\\]\n\\[\n3790 + 45 = 3835\n\\]\n\nVerification using another method (using the formula \\((a + b)(c + d) = ac + ad + bc + bd\\)) confirms the result:\n\n\\[\n59 \\times 65 = (50 + 9)(60 + 5) = 50 \\times 60 + 50 \\times 5 + 9 \\times 60 + 9 \\times 5 = 3000 + 250 + 540 + 45 = 3835\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of \\(59\\) and \\(65\\) is \\(\\boxed{3835}\\).\n\n<|end_of_solution|>",
