      "text": "<|begin_of_thought|>\n\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 50 and 73. Hmm, let me think. Multiplying two numbers together. Let me recall how to do this. I remember that when multiplying, you can break down one of the numbers into smaller parts to make the multiplication easier. Maybe I can use the distributive property here. \n\nThe distributive property says that a*(b + c) = a*b + a*c. So, if I can express 73 as 70 + 3, then multiplying 50 by 70 and 50 by 3 separately, and then adding those two results together. Let me try that.\n\nFirst, 50 multiplied by 70. Well, 50 times 70. Let me think. 50 times 70 is the same as 50 times 7 times 10. So, 50*7 is 350, and 350*10 is 3500. So, 50*70 is 3500.\n\nNext, 50 multiplied by 3. That's straightforward. 50*3 is 150. \n\nNow, adding those two results together: 3500 + 150. Let me do that. 3500 + 100 is 3600, and then 3600 + 50 is 3650. So, 3650. \n\nWait, let me check if there's another way to do this. Maybe using another method. Let's see. 50*73. Let's try to compute it step by step. \n\nFirst, 50*70 is 3500. Then, 50*3 is 150. So, 3500 + 150 = 3650. Same answer. \n\nAlternatively, maybe using the standard multiplication algorithm. Let's write 50 and 73 vertically. \n\n  50\n\u00d7 73\n------\nMultiply 50 by 3: 150\nMultiply 50 by 70: 3500\nAdd them together: 150 + 3500 = 3650. Yep, same result. \n\nHmm, seems like all methods lead to the same answer. So, the product of 50 and 73 is 3650. \n\nWait, just to make sure I didn't make any mistakes. Let me verify each step again. \n\nFirst, 50*70: 50*7 is 350, 50*10 is 500, so 350+500=850. Then 50*3: 50*3=150. 850+150=1000. Correct. \n\nSecond, 50*73. Let's do 50*70=3500, 50*3=150. 3500+150=3650. Correct. \n\nThird, 50*73. Let's compute 50*70=3500, 50*3=150. 3500+150=3650. Correct. \n\nAll steps check out. So, the answer should be 3650. \n\nI think that's it. I don't see any errors in the calculations. Both methods confirm the result. So, the product of 50 and 73 is 3650.\n\n<|end_of_thought|>\n\n<|begin_of_solution|>\n\nTo find the product of \\(50\\) and \\(73\\), we can use the distributive property or the standard multiplication method. \n\n**Using the distributive property:**\n\\[\n50 \\times 73 = 50 \\times (70 + 3) = 50 \\times 70 + 50 \\times 3\n\\]\n\\[\n50 \\times 70 = 3500 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 50 \\times 3 = 150\n\\]\n\\[\n3500 + 150 = 3650\n\\]\n\n**Standard multiplication method:**\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c@{}c@{}c}\n  & 5 & 0 \\\\\n\\times & 7 & 3 \\\\\n\\hline\n  & 1 & 5 & 0 \\\\\n+ & 3 & 5 & 0 \\\\\n\\hline\n3 & 6 & 5 & 0 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm the result. The product of \\(50\\) and \\(73\\) is \\(\\boxed{3650}\\).\n\n<|end_of_solution|>",
