      "text": "<|begin_of_thought|>\n\nOkay, let's see. The question is asking for the product of 77 and 10. Hmm, product means multiplication, right? So I need to multiply 77 by 10. Let me think about how to do this. \n\nFirst, I remember that when you multiply a number by 10, you just add a zero to the end of that number. So, if I take 77 and add a zero, that should give me the product. Let me check that. 77 times 10 is 770. Yeah, that seems right. \n\nWait, maybe I should verify this another way to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Let's break it down. 77 times 10 is the same as 77 multiplied by 10. If I think of 10 as 10 ones, then 77 times 10 would be 77 times 10 ones. That would be 77 tens, which is 770. Yep, that matches. \n\nAlternatively, I can use the distributive property. 77 times 10 can be written as 70 times 10 plus 7 times 10. 70 times 10 is 700, and 7 times 10 is 70. Adding those together gives 700 + 70 = 770. That's another way to confirm the answer. \n\nI think all these methods lead to the same result, so I can be confident that the product of 77 and 10 is 770. Let me just recap to make sure I didn't skip any steps. The problem is straightforward: multiply 77 by 10. The key is recognizing that multiplying by 10 is equivalent to adding a zero to the end of the number. Since 77 has two digits, adding a zero makes it 770. All checks confirm this is correct. \n\nNo, I don't see any errors here. The multiplication is straightforward, and the reasoning is solid. So, the final answer should be 770.\n\n<|end_of_thought|>\n\n<|begin_of_solution|>\n\nThe product of 77 and 10 is calculated by recognizing that multiplying by 10 adds a zero to the end of the number. Thus, \\(77 \\times 10 = 770\\). This can also be verified using the distributive property: \\(77 \\times 10 = 70 \\times 10 + 7 \\times 10 = 700 + 70 = 770\\). \n\n\\boxed{770}\n\n<|end_of_solution|>",
