      "text": "<|begin_of_thought|>\n\nOkay, let's see. I need to find the product of 78 and 64. Hmm, multiplication. Let me think. Maybe I can break it down into smaller parts. \n\nFirst, 78 times 64. Maybe I can use the distributive property here. That means breaking down one of the numbers into parts that are easier to multiply. Let's take 78. If I split 78 into 70 and 8, that might make the multiplication simpler. \n\nSo, 78 times 64 becomes (70 + 8) times 64. Now, I can apply the distributive property again. That would be (70 times 64) plus (8 times 64). \n\nLet me calculate each part separately. Starting with 70 times 64. Well, 70 times 60 is 4200, and 70 times 4 is 280. Adding those together gives 4200 + 280 = 4480. \n\nNow, 8 times 64. Let's see, 64 times 8. Hmm, 60 times 8 is 480, and 4 times 8 is 32. Adding those gives 480 + 32 = 512. \n\nSo, putting it all together, 4480 plus 512. Let me add those numbers. 4480 + 500 is 4980, and then +12 is 4992. \n\nWait, let me check that again. 4480 + 512. 4480 + 500 is 4980, and 4980 + 12 is 4992. Yeah, that seems right. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can do it another way. Let's see, 78 times 64. Let's try multiplying 70 times 64 first, which is 4480, and then 8 times 64 is 512. Adding those gives 4480 + 512 = 4992. \n\nHmm, both methods give the same result. So, the product of 78 and 64 is 4992. \n\nWait, let me verify once more. 78 times 64. Let's compute it step by step. \n\n78 times 60 is 4680. 78 times 4 is 312. Adding those together: 4680 + 312. Let's do 4680 + 300 = 4980, then +12 = 4992. Yep, that's correct. \n\nAlternatively, 78 times 64. Let's compute 70 times 64 first. 70 times 60 is 4200, 70 times 4 is 280. 4200 + 280 = 4480. Then 8 times 64 is 512. 4480 + 512 = 4992. \n\nSo, both methods confirm that the product is 4992. \n\nI think that's solid. Let me just check if there's any chance I made a mistake in the multiplication. Let's take 78 times 64. \n\n78 times 60: 70 times 60 is 4200, 8 times 60 is 480. 4200 + 480 = 4680. \n\n78 times 4: 70 times 4 is 280, 8 times 4 is 32. 280 + 32 = 312. \n\nAdding 4680 + 312: 4680 + 300 = 4980, 4980 + 12 = 4992. Yep, that's correct. \n\nAlternatively, 78 times 64. Let's compute 70 times 64 first. 70 times 60 is 4200, 70 times 4 is 280. 4200 + 280 = 4480. Then 8 times 64 is 512. 4480 + 512 = 4992. \n\nSo, both methods give the same result. \n\nTherefore, the product of 78 and 64 is 4992. \n\n<|end_of_thought|>\n\n<|begin_of_solution|>\n\nTo find the product of \\(78 \\times 64\\), we can use the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n78 \\times 64 = (70 + 8) \\times 64 = 70 \\times 64 + 8 \\times 64\n\\]\n\nCalculating each part separately:\n\n1. \\(70 \\times 64\\):\n   \\[\n   70 \\times 60 = 4200 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 70 \\times 4 = 280\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   4200 + 280 = 4480\n   \\]\n\n2. \\(8 \\times 64\\):\n   \\[\n   60 \\times 8 = 480 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 4 \\times 8 = 32\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   480 + 32 = 512\n   \\]\n\nAdding the results:\n\\[\n4480 + 512 = 4992\n\\]\n\nVerification by direct multiplication:\n\\[\n78 \\times 64 = 70 \\times 64 + 8 \\times 64 = 4480 + 512 = 4992\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of \\(78 \\times 64\\) is \\(\\boxed{4992}\\).\n\n<|end_of_solution|>",
