 sure there's no trick here. The question is straightforward multiplication. There's no need to convert to another base or anything like that. So, 25 times 73 is indeed 1825.\n\n<|end_of_thought|>\n\n<|begin_of_solution|>\n\nThe product of 25 and 73 is calculated by multiplying each digit of one number by the corresponding digit of the other number and then adding the results. Breaking it down:\n\n1. **25 \u00d7 70**:  \n   \\(25 \\times 7 = 175\\), then \\(175 \\times 10 = 1750\\).\n\n2. **25 \u00d7 3**:  \n   \\(25 \\times 3 = 75\\).\n\n3. **Adding the results**:  \n   \\(1750 + 75 = 1825\\).\n\nVerification using the distributive property:  \n\\(25 \\times (70 + 3) = 25 \\times 70 + 25 \\times 3 = 1750 + 75 = 1825\\).\n\nThus, the final answer is \\(\\boxed{1825}\\).\n\n<|end_of_solution|>",
