      "text": "<|begin_of_thought|>\n\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 23 and 65. Hmm, let me think. Multiplying two numbers together. I remember that when multiplying, you can use the standard multiplication method, which involves multiplying each digit of one number by each digit of the other number and then adding those products together. Let me try that step by step.\n\nFirst, let's write down the numbers vertically. 23 is the first number, and 65 is the second. So, 23 \u00d7 65. I can break this down into two parts: 20 \u00d7 65 and 3 \u00d7 65. That might make it easier to compute each part separately and then add them together.\n\nStarting with 20 \u00d7 65. Let's calculate that. 20 times 65. Well, 20 times 60 is 1200, and 20 times 5 is 100. So, adding those together gives 1200 + 100 = 1300. Okay, so 20 \u00d7 65 is 1300.\n\nNow, moving on to 3 \u00d7 65. Let's do that. 3 times 60 is 180, and 3 times 5 is 15. Adding those gives 180 + 15 = 195. So, 3 \u00d7 65 is 195.\n\nNow, to find the total product, I need to add 1300 and 195. Let me do that. 1300 + 195. Hmm, 1300 + 100 is 1400, and then 195 is 195, so 1400 + 195. Let me compute that. 1400 + 100 is 1500, and then 1500 + 95 is 1595. So, 1300 + 195 equals 1595.\n\nWait, let me double-check my calculations to make sure I didn't make any mistakes. For 20 \u00d7 65: 20 \u00d7 60 is 1200, 20 \u00d7 5 is 100. 1200 + 100 is 1300. That seems right. For 3 \u00d7 65: 3 \u00d7 60 is 180, 3 \u00d7 5 is 15. 180 + 15 is 195. That's correct. Adding 1300 and 195: 1300 + 100 is 1400, 1400 + 95 is 1595. Yep, that's right.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method to verify. Let's see, 23 \u00d7 65. Let's try another approach. Maybe using the distributive property. 23 \u00d7 65 = 20 \u00d7 65 + 3 \u00d7 65. Which we already calculated as 1300 + 195 = 1595. That's the same result. So that's another way to check.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the standard multiplication algorithm again. Let's try that. 23 \u00d