      "text": "<|begin_of_thought|>\n\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 30 and 42. Hmm, let me think. Multiplying two numbers together. Let me recall how to do that. I remember that when you multiply two numbers, you can break them down into smaller parts. Maybe I can use the distributive property here. \n\nThe distributive property says that a*(b + c) = a*b + a*c. So, if I can express 42 as 30 + 12, then multiplying 30 by 42 would be 30*(30 + 12). Let me compute that. \n\nFirst, 30 times 30 is 900. Then, 30 times 12. Let me calculate that. 30 times 10 is 300, and 30 times 2 is 60. Adding those together gives 360. So, 30*12 is 360. \n\nNow, adding those two results together: 900 + 360. Let me do that. 900 + 300 is 1200, and 360 is 360. So, 1200 + 360 is 1560. \n\nWait, let me check if there's another way to do this. Maybe using another method. Let's see. 42 is 30 + 12, so 30*42 = 30*(30 + 12) = 30*30 + 30*12. Which is 900 + 360 = 1260. \n\nHmm, so both methods give me 1260. That seems right. Let me verify with another approach. Maybe using the standard multiplication algorithm. \n\n30  \n\u00d7 42  \n------  \nFirst, multiply 30 by 2: 30*2=60.  \nThen, multiply 30 by 40: 30*40=1200.  \nNow, add those two results: 60 + 1200 = 1260. Yep, same answer. \n\nAlternatively, maybe using the fact that 42 is 30 + 12, so 30*(30 + 12) = 30*30 + 30*12. That's the same as before. \n\nI think that's solid. So, the product of 30 and 42 is 1260. Let me just make sure there's no mistake in the calculation. \n\n30*40 is 1200, 30*2 is 60. 1200 + 60 is 1260. Yep, that's correct. \n\nAlternatively, if I use the distributive property again, 30*(30 + 12) = 30*30 + 30*12. 30*30 is 900, 30*12 is 360. 900 + 360 is 1260. \n\nAnother way: 42 is 30 + 12, so 30*(30 + 12) = 30*30 + 30*12. 30*30 is 900, 30*12 is 360. 900 + 360 is 1260. \n\nAll methods lead to the same answer. So, I can be confident that the product of 30 and 42 is 1260. \n\n<|end_of_thought|>\n\n<|begin_of_solution|>\n\nThe product of 30 and 42 is calculated as follows:\n\n\\[\n30 \\times 42 = 30 \\times (30 + 12) = 30 \\times 30 + 30 \\times 12 = 900 + 360 = 1260\n\\]\n\nVerification using the distributive property confirms the result. Alternatively, breaking down 42 into 30 + 12 and multiplying each part by 30 gives the same result. \n\n\\boxed{1260}\n\n<|end_of_solution|>",
