 860, then add 9 more, so 869. Correct. \n\nAlternatively, if I use the distributive property, 11*(70 + 9) = 11*70 + 11*9 = 770 + 99 = 869. Yep, that's another way to see it. \n\nSo I feel confident that the answer is 869. No steps seem to be missing or incorrect. All methods converge to the same result. Therefore, the product of 11 and 79 is 869.\n\n<|end_of_thought|>\n\n<|begin_of_solution|>\n\nTo find the product of \\(11\\) and \\(79\\), we can use the distributive property or the standard multiplication method. \n\n**Using the distributive property:**\n\\[\n11 \\times 79 = 11 \\times (70 + 9) = 11 \\times 70 + 11 \\times 9\n\\]\nCalculating each term:\n\\[\n11 \\times 70 = 770 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 11 \\times 9 = 99\n\\]\nAdding these results:\n\\[\n770 + 99 = 869\n\\]\n\n**Standard multiplication method:**\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c@{}c@{}c}\n & 1 & 1 \\\\\n\\times & 7 & 9 \\\\\n\\hline\n & 9 & 9 \\\\\n+ & 7 & 7 & 0 \\\\\n\\hline\n8 & 6 & 9 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm the product is \\(869\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{869}\n\\]\n\n<|end_of_solution|>",
