      "index": 0,
      "text": "<|begin_of_thought|>\n\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 90 and 31. Hmm, let me think. Multiplying two numbers together. Let me recall how to do this. \n\nFirst, I remember that when multiplying a two-digit number by a one-digit number, you can break down the multiplication into simpler parts. Maybe I can use the distributive property here. Let me try that. \n\nSo, 90 multiplied by 31. I can split 90 into 90 and 10, right? Because 90 is 90 + 10. Then, multiplying each part by 31 and adding the results together. That should work. Let me check that. \n\nSo, 90 * 31 = (90 * 30) + (90 * 1) = 2700 + 90 = 2790. Wait, is that right? Let me verify. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can do it another way. Let's see. 31 times 90. Well, 31 times 90 is the same as 31 times 90. Let me compute that. \n\n31 times 90. Let's break it down. 31 times 90. 31 times 9 is 279, and 31 times 10 is 310. So, 279 + 310. Adding those together: 279 + 310. Let's see, 279 + 300 is 579, and then +10 is 589. So, 31 times 90 is 2790. \n\nWait, both methods give me 2790. That seems consistent. So, the product of 90 and 31 is 2790. \n\nBut let me just make sure I didn't make any mistakes in the steps. Let's go through the multiplication again. \n\n90 multiplied by 31. 90 times 30 is 2700, and 90 times 1 is 90. Adding those together gives 2700 + 90 = 2790. Yep, that's correct. \n\nAlternatively, if I use the distributive property, 90*31 = 90*(30 + 1) = 90*30 + 90*1 = 2700 + 90 = 2790. Same result. \n\nAnother way to check is to use estimation. 90 times 30 is 2700, and 90 times 1 is 90. Adding those gives 2790. That seems reasonable. \n\nWait, but let me think if there's another way to approach this. Maybe using the standard multiplication algorithm. Let's try that. \n\n90  \n\u00d7 31  \n------  \nFirst, multiply 90 by 1 (the ones place of 31). That's 90.  \nThen, multiply 90 by 30 (the tens place of 31). That's 90*30=2700.  \nNow, add those two results together: 90 + 2700 = 2790. Same answer. \n\nSo, all methods lead to the same result. Therefore, I can be confident that the product of 90 and 31 is 2790. \n\nJust to recap, breaking down the numbers into parts, using the distributive property, checking with estimation, and verifying with the standard multiplication algorithm all confirm that the product is indeed 2790. \n\nI don't see any errors in the calculations. Each step checks out. So, I think that's the correct answer. \n\n<|end_of_thought|>\n\n<|begin_of_solution|>\n\nTo find the product of \\(90\\) and \\(31\\), we can use the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n90 \\times 31 = 90 \\times (30 + 1) = 90 \\times 30 + 90 \\times 1\n\\]\n\nCalculating each part:\n\n\\[\n90 \\times 30 = 2700 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 90 \\times 1 = 90\n\\]\n\nAdding these results together:\n\n\\[\n2700 + 90 = 2790\n\\]\n\nVerification using estimation:\n\n\\[\n90 \\times 30 = 2700 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 90 \\times 1 = 90 \\quad \\text{so} \\quad 2700 + 90 = 2790\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm the product is \\(\\boxed{2790}\\).\n\n<|end_of_solution|>",
