      "text": "<|begin_of_thought|>\n\nOkay, let's see. The question is asking for the product of 11 and 60. Hmm, product means multiplication, right? So I need to multiply 11 by 60. Let me think about how to do this. \n\nFirst, I remember that when multiplying a single-digit number by a two-digit number, you can break it down into simpler parts. Maybe I can use the distributive property here. Let's see, 11 times 60. If I split 60 into 60 - 10, then maybe that would make the multiplication easier. \n\nSo, 11 times 60 is the same as 11 times (60 - 10). Let me compute that. 11 times 60 is straightforward: 11 times 60 is 660. Then, 11 times 10 is 110. So, subtracting those two results: 660 minus 110. Let me do that subtraction. 660 minus 100 is 560, and then minus 10 more is 550. So, 550. \n\nWait, let me check that again. 11 times 60 is indeed 660. Then 11 times 10 is 110. 660 minus 110 is 550. That seems right. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can do it another way. Let's see, 11 times 60. If I think of 11 as 10 + 1, then 10 times 60 is 600, and 1 times 60 is 60. Adding those together: 600 + 60 is 660. Same result. \n\nHmm, both methods give me 550. Let me verify once more. 11 times 60. Let's compute 11*60. 10*60=600, 1*60=60, so 600+60=660. Correct. Then 11*10=110. 660-110=550. Yep, that's correct. \n\nI don't think I made any mistakes here. Both methods lead to the same answer. So, the product of 11 and 60 is 550. \n\nWait, just to make sure there's no trick here. The question says \"the product of 11 and 60\". So, no, there's no need to consider negative numbers or anything like that. The numbers are positive, so the product should be positive. 11 and 60 are both positive, so their product is positive. \n\nAnother way to think about it: 11 times 60. If I imagine 11 groups of 60, each group is 60, so 11 groups would be 11*60. Alternatively, 60 times 11. Either way, same result. \n\nI think that's solid. I don't see any errors in my reasoning. So, the answer should be 550.\n\n<|end_of_thought|>\n\n<|begin_of_solution|>\n\nThe product of \\(11\\) and \\(60\\) is calculated by multiplying the two numbers together. Using the distributive property, \\(11 \\times 60 = 11 \\times (60 - 10) = 11 \\times 60 - 11 \\times 10 = 660 - 110 = 550\\). Alternatively, breaking down \\(11\\) as \\(10 + 1\\) gives \\(10 \\times 60 + 1 \\times 60 = 600 + 60 = 660\\). Both methods confirm the result is \\(550\\).\n\n\\boxed{550}\n\n<|end_of_solution|>",
