      "index": 0,
      "text": "<|begin_of_thought|>\n\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 85 and 85. Hmm, let me think. Multiplying two numbers together. Let me recall how to do that. I remember that when you multiply two numbers, you can use the distributive property or just multiply them directly. Let me try both methods to make sure I get the right answer.\n\nFirst method: Direct multiplication. So, 85 times 85. Let me write that out. 85 times 85. Maybe I can break it down. 85 times 80 is 6,800, and 85 times 5 is 425. Then add those two results together: 6,800 + 425. Let me calculate that. 6,800 + 400 is 7,200, and then 7,200 + 25 is 7,225. So, 85 times 85 is 7,225. That seems straightforward.\n\nSecond method: Using the distributive property. Let's see. 85 times 85. Maybe I can express 85 as 80 + 5. Then, 80 times 85 is 6,800, and 5 times 85 is 425. Adding those two gives 6,800 + 425. Same result as before. So, that's another way to check. 6,800 + 425 is indeed 7,225. So, both methods give the same answer. That gives me more confidence that 7,225 is correct.\n\nWait, let me just verify once more. Maybe I can use another approach. Let's compute 85 squared. 85 times 85. Let's see, 85 squared is 7,225. Yep, that's the same. So, that's a good way to confirm. Squaring a number is the same as multiplying it by itself. So, 85 times 85 is 7,225. That's a quick way to check. So, all methods lead to the same answer. Therefore, I can be sure that the product of 85 and 85 is 7,225.\n\n<|end_of_thought|>\n\n<|begin_of_solution|>\n\nThe product of 85 and 85 is calculated using the direct multiplication method:  \n\\[\n85 \\times 85 = 85 \\times (80 + 5) = 85 \\times 80 + 85 \\times 5 = 6,800 + 425 = 7,225.\n\\]  \nAlternatively, using the distributive property:  \n\\[\n85 \\times 85 = (80 + 5)^2 = 80^2 + 2 \\times 80 \\times 5 + 5^2 = 6,400 + 800 + 25 = 7,225.\n\\]  \nBoth methods confirm the result.  \n\\boxed{7225}\n\n<|end_of_solution|>",
