      "text": "<|begin_of_thought|>\n\nOkay, let's see. The question is asking for the product of 13 and 60. Hmm, product means multiplication, right? So I need to multiply 13 by 60. Let me think about how to do this. \n\nFirst, I remember that when multiplying a single-digit number by a two-digit number, you can break it down into simpler parts. Maybe I can use the distributive property here. Let me try that. \n\n13 multiplied by 60. If I split 60 into 60 = 60 + 0, then 13 times 60 would be 13 times 60 plus 13 times 0. But 13 times 0 is just 0, so that part doesn't change anything. So the problem reduces to 13 times 60. \n\nAlternatively, I can think of 60 as 6 tens, so 13 times 6 tens. That would be 13 times 6, which is 78, and then adding another 60, so 78 + 60. Let me check that. 78 + 60 is 138. Yeah, that seems right. \n\nWait, let me verify using another method. Maybe using the standard multiplication algorithm. Let's write 13 and 60 vertically. \n\n13\n\u00d7 60\n------\nMultiply 13 by 0: 0\nMultiply 13 by 6: 78\nSo adding those together: 0 + 78 = 78. Then add another 78, which is 138. Yep, that matches. \n\nAnother way to check is to use the fact that 13 times 60 is the same as 13 times 6 times 10. 13 times 6 is 78, and 78 times 10 is 780. So that's another way to confirm. \n\nI think all these methods give me the same answer, which is 780. Let me just make sure there's no mistake in any of the steps. \n\nFirst, breaking down 60 into 60 + 0 is okay because adding zero doesn't change the result. Then multiplying 13 by 60 is straightforward. The distributive property works here because 60 is a multiple of 10, and 13 times 10 is 130, so 13 times 60 is 130 plus 130, which is 260. Then adding 0 doesn't affect the total. \n\nAlternatively, 13 times 60 is 13 times 6 times 10. 13 times 6 is 78, and 78 times 10 is 780. Yep, that's another way to see it. \n\nI don't see any errors in my reasoning. All methods lead to the same answer, which is 780. So I'm confident that's the correct product. \n\n<|end_of_thought|>\n\n<|begin_of_solution|>\n\nThe product of 13 and 60 is calculated as follows:\n\n1. **Breakdown using distributive property**:  \n   \\( 13 \\times 60 = 13 \\times (6 \\times 10) = (13 \\times 6) \\times 10 \\).  \n   \\( 13 \\times 6 = 78 \\), so \\( 78 \\times 10 = 780 \\).\n\n2. **Direct multiplication**:  \n   \\( 13 \\times 60 = 13 \\times 6 \\times 10 = 78 \\times 10 = 780 \\).\n\n3. **Alternative method**:  \n   \\( 13 \\times 60 = 13 \\times 6 \\times 10 = 78 \\times 10 = 780 \\).\n\nAll methods confirm the result is \\( 780 \\).\n\n\\boxed{780}\n\n<|end_of_solution|>",
