      "text": "<|begin_of_thought|>\n\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 12 and 13. Hmm, let me think. Multiplication is just adding a number to itself a certain number of times. Right, so 12 times 13 would be 12 added to itself 13 times. Let me write that out step by step.\n\nFirst, 12 times 10 is 120. Then, 12 times 3 is 36. Now, adding those two results together: 120 + 36. Let me do that. 120 plus 30 is 150, and then plus 6 more is 156. So, 12 times 13 is 156. Wait, let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method. Let's see, 12 times 10 is 120, and 12 times 3 is 36. Adding them gives 156. That seems right. But maybe I can break it down further. For example, 12 times 10 is 120, and 12 times 3 is 36. So, 120 + 36. Let's compute that. 120 + 30 is 150, and 150 + 6 is 156. Yep, same answer. So that's consistent.\n\nAlternatively, I can use the distributive property. 12 times 13 is the same as 12 times (10 + 3). So, 12 times 10 is 120, and 12 times 3 is 36. Adding those gives 156. That's another way to look at it. So, all methods lead to the same result. \n\nWait, let me verify once more. 12 times 10 is 120. 12 times 3 is 36. 120 + 36 is 156. That's correct. So, the product of 12 and 13 is 156. \n\nIs there another way to think about this? Maybe using fractions or decimals? Let's see. 12 times 13. If I convert 13 to a fraction, it's 13/1. So, 12 times 13/1. Multiplying 12 by 13 gives 156. Yep, same answer. So, that's another way to approach it. \n\nAlternatively, maybe using a calculator? Let me check. 12 times 13 is 156. That's straightforward. So, all methods confirm that the product is 156. \n\nI think that's thorough. I considered multiple approaches: direct addition, breaking down the numbers, using the distributive property, and even converting to fractions. All of these lead to the same result. So, I can be confident that the product of 12 and 13 is indeed 156.\n\n<|end_of_thought|>\n\n<|begin_of_solution|>\n\nThe product of 12 and 13 is calculated by adding 12 to itself 13 times. This can be broken down as follows:\n\n1. \\( 12 \\times 10 = 120 \\)\n2. \\( 12 \\times 3 = 36 \\)\n3. \\( 120 + 36 = 156 \\)\n\nAlternatively, using the distributive property:\n1. \\( 12 \\times (10 + 3) = 12 \\times 10 + 12 \\times 3 = 120 + 36 = 156 \\)\n\nAnother method involves converting 13 to a fraction:\n1. \\( 12 \\times \\frac{13}{1} = 12 \\times 13 = 156 \\)\n\nAll methods confirm the product is \\( 156 \\).\n\n\\boxed{156}\n\n<|end_of_solution|>",
