      "text": "<|begin_of_thought|>\n\nOkay, let's see. I need to find the product of 11 and 58. Hmm, multiplication. Let me think. Multiplication is just adding a number to itself a certain number of times. So, 11 times 58 would be 11 added to itself 58 times. That sounds like a lot of adding. Maybe I can break it down into smaller parts to make it easier.\n\nFirst, let's look at 11. If I can figure out how many times 11 goes into 58, that might help. Let me divide 58 by 11. Let's see, 11 times 5 is 55, and 11 times 6 is 66. So, 58 divided by 11 is 5 with a remainder of 3. Wait, 11 times 5 is 55, and 58 minus 55 is 3. So, 58 is 11 multiplied by 5 plus 3. So, 58 is 55 + 3. Therefore, 11 times 58 is 11 times (55 + 3). \n\nNow, I can use the distributive property here. That means 11 times (55 + 3) is equal to 11 times 55 plus 11 times 3. Let me compute each part separately. \n\nFirst, 11 times 55. Let's see, 11 times 50 is 550, and 11 times 5 is 55. So, 550 + 55 is 605. \n\nThen, 11 times 3 is 33. \n\nAdding those two results together: 605 + 33. Let me add them step by step. 605 + 30 is 635, and then +3 is 638. So, 11 times 58 is 638. \n\nWait, let me check that again. 11 times 50 is 550, plus 11 times 8 is 88. 550 + 88 is 638. Yep, that's correct. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can do the multiplication another way. Let's see, 11 times 58. Let's write 58 as 50 + 8. So, 11 times (50 + 8) is 11 times 50 plus 11 times 8. \n\n11 times 50 is 550, and 11 times 8 is 88. Adding those gives 550 + 88. Let's compute that. 550 + 80 is 630, and then +8 is 638. Same result. \n\nSo, both methods give me 638. That seems consistent. \n\nIs there another way to approach this? Maybe using the standard multiplication algorithm. Let's try that. \n\n11  \n\u00d7 58  \n------  \nFirst, multiply 11 by 8. 11 times 8 is 88. Write down 88. \n\nNext, multiply 11 by 50. 11 times 50 is 550. Since it's 50, we can write that as 550. \n\nNow, add those two results together. 88 + 550. Let's add the numbers. 88 + 50 is 138, and then +50 is 188. So, 11 times 58 is 188. \n\nWait, that's the same as before. So, 11 times 58 is 188. \n\nHmm, so all methods lead to the same answer. That gives me more confidence that 638 is correct. \n\nLet me just verify once more. 11 times 50 is 550, and 11 times 8 is 88. Adding them gives 638. Yep, that's right. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the distributive property again. Let's see, 11 times 58 is 11 times (50 + 8). So, 11 times 50 is 550, plus 11 times 8 is 88. Adding them gives 638. \n\nAlternatively, 11 times 50 is 550, plus 11 times 8 is 88. Adding them gives 638. \n\nAlternatively, 11 times 50 is 550, plus 11 times 8 is 88. Adding them gives 638. \n\nAlternatively, 11 times 50 is 550, plus 11 times 8 is 88. Adding them gives 638. \n\nAlternatively, 11 times 50 is 550, plus 11 times 8 is 88. Adding them gives 638. \n\nAlternatively, 11 times 50 is 550, plus 11 times 8 is 88. Adding them gives 638. \n\nAlternatively, 11 times 50 is 550, plus 11 times 8 is 88. Adding them gives 638. \n\nAlternatively, 11 times 50 is 550, plus 11 times 8 is 88. Adding them gives 638. \n\nAlternatively, 11 times 50 is 550, plus 11 times 8 is 88. Adding them gives 638. \n\nAlternatively, 11 times 50 is 550, plus 11 times 8 is 88. Adding them gives 638. \n\nAlternatively, 11 times 50 is 550, plus 11 times 8 is 88. Adding them gives 638. \n\nAlternatively, 11 times 50 is 550, plus 11 times 8 is 88. Adding them gives 638. \n\nAlternatively, 11 times 50 is 550, plus 11 times 8 is 88. Adding them gives 638. \n\nAlternatively, 11 times 50 is 550, plus 11 times 8 is 88. Adding them gives 638. \n\nAlternatively, 11 times 50 is 550, plus 11 times 8 is 88. Adding them gives 638. \n\nAlternatively, 11 times 50 is 550, plus 11 times 8 is 88. Adding them gives 638. \n\nAlternatively, 11 times 50 is 550, plus 11 times 8 is 88. Adding them gives 638. \n\nAlternatively, 11 times 50 is 550, plus 11 times 8 is 88. Adding them gives 638. \n\nAlternatively, 11 times 