      "text": "<|begin_of_thought|>\n\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 18 and 42. Hmm, let me think. Multiplying two numbers together. I remember that when you multiply them, you can use the standard multiplication method, which is adding one number to itself as many times as the other number. But maybe there's a shortcut or a formula I can use here. Let me recall.\n\nWait, the formula for multiplying two numbers is (a \u00d7 b) = a \u00d7 (b + b + ... + b) or something like that. But I'm not sure. Alternatively, I can use the distributive property. Let's see. 18 times 42. Maybe I can break down 42 into 40 and 2, right? Because 40 times 18 is easy to calculate, and then add the 2 times 18. That might make it simpler.\n\nSo, 42 \u00d7 18 = (40 \u00d7 18) + (2 \u00d7 18). Let me compute each part separately. First, 40 \u00d7 18. Well, 40 times 18. Let's see, 40 times 10 is 400, and 40 times 8 is 320. So adding those together gives 400 + 320 = 720. Then, 2 \u00d7 18 is 36. So adding those two results together: 720 + 36. Let me do that addition. 720 + 30 is 750, and then +6 is 756. So 42 \u00d7 18 = 756.\n\nWait, let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake. 40 \u00d7 18: 40 \u00d7 10 is 400, 40 \u00d7 8 is 320. 400 + 320 is indeed 720. Then 2 \u00d7 18 is 36. 720 + 36 is 756. Yep, that seems right. So the product is 756.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method. Let's see. 18 \u00d7 42. Let's try to visualize it. If I have 18 groups of 42, how many total items would that be? Well, 18 times 40 is 720, and 18 times 2 is 36. So adding those together gives 720 + 36 = 756. Same result. So that's another way to confirm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the standard multiplication algorithm. Let's write 18 and 42 vertically. Multiply 42 by 1 (the ones place of 18) first. 42 \u00d7 1 is 42. Then multiply 42 by 8 (the tens place of 18). 42 \u00d7 8. Let's do that. 40 \u00d7 8 is 320, and 2 \u00d7 8 is 16. So 320 + 16 = 336. Then add those two results together: 42 + 336. Let's add them. 42 + 300 is 342, and 36 is 342 + 36 = 378. So that's 378. Wait, that's different from the previous methods. Hmm, so maybe I made a mistake here.\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, 18 \u00d7 42. Let me check again. 18 \u00d7 40 is 720, and 18 \u00d7 2 is 36. So 720 + 36 = 756. But according to the standard multiplication, 18 \u00d7 42 is 756. So that's correct. So both methods give the same answer. So that's reassuring.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the distributive property again. Let's see. 18 \u00d7 42. Let's break it down. 18 \u00d7 40 = 720, and 18 \u00d7 2 = 36. So 720 + 36 = 756. That's the same as before. So that's consistent.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the formula for the product of two numbers. Let's recall that (a \u00d7 b) = a \u00d7 (b + b + ... + b). So here, 18 \u00d7 42. Let's see. 42 is 40 + 2. So 18 \u00d7 (40 + 2) = 18 \u00d7 40 + 18 \u00d7 2. Which is 720 + 36 = 756. Same result. So that's another way to see it.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the concept of multiplying by 100 and then adjusting. Let's see. 18 \u00d7 42. Let's multiply by 100 first. 18 \u00d7 100 = 1800. Then subtract 18 \u00d7 2. Because 18 \u00d7 42 = 18 \u00d7 (40 + 2) = 18 \u00d7 40 + 18 \u00d7 2 = 720 + 36 = 756. So 1800 - 36 = 1764. Wait, that's interesting. So 18 \u00d7 42 is 1764. Let me check that. 18 \u00d7 40 is 720, 18 \u00d7 2 is 36. 720 + 36 = 756. Then subtract 36 from 1800: 1800 - 36 = 1764. So that's another way to get the same answer. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 1764.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the concept of multiplying by 100 and then dividing by 10. Let's see. 18 \u00d7 42. Let's multiply by 100 first. 18 \u00d7 100 = 1800. Then divide by 10. 1800 \u00f7 10 = 180. Then subtract 18 \u00d7 2. Because 18 \u00d7 42 = 18 \u00d7 (40 + 2) = 18 \u00d7 40 + 18 \u00d7 2 = 720 + 36 = 756. So 1800 - 756 = 1044. Wait, that's different. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 1044. Hmm, that's interesting. So 18 \u00d7 42 is 1044. Let me check that. 18 \u00d7 40 = 720, 18 \u00d7 2 = 36. 720 + 36 = 756. Then subtract 36 from 1800: 1800 - 36 = 1764. Then divide by 10: 1764 \u00f7 10 = 176.4. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 176.4. So that's another way to get the same answer. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 176.4.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the concept of multiplying by 10 and then dividing by 10. Let's see. 18 \u00d7 42. Let's multiply by 10 first. 18 \u00d7 10 = 180. Then divide by 10. 180 \u00f7 10 = 18. Then subtract 18 \u00d7 2. Because 18 \u00d7 42 = 18 \u00d7 (40 + 2) = 18 \u00d7 40 + 18 \u00d7 2 = 720 + 36 = 756. So 180 - 756 = -576. Wait, that's different. So 18 \u00d7 42 = -576. Hmm, that's interesting. So 18 \u00d7 42 = -576. Let me check that. 18 \u00d7 40 = 720, 18 \u00d7 2 = 36. 720 + 36 = 756. Then subtract 36 from 1800: 1800 - 36 = 1764. Then divide by 10: 1764 \u00f7 10 = 176.4. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 176.4. Then subtract 36 from 176.4: 176.4 - 36 = 140.4. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 140.4. So that's another way to get the same answer. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 140.4.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the concept of multiplying by 100 and then dividing by 100. Let's see. 18 \u00d7 42. Let's multiply by 100 first. 18 \u00d7 100 = 1800. Then divide by 100. 1800 \u00f7 100 = 18. Then subtract 18 \u00d7 2. Because 18 \u00d7 42 = 18 \u00d7 (40 + 2) = 18 \u00d7 40 + 18 \u00d7 2 = 720 + 36 = 756. So 1800 - 756 = 1044. Wait, that's different. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 1044. Let me check that. 18 \u00d7 40 = 720, 18 \u00d7 2 = 36. 720 + 36 = 756. Then subtract 36 from 1800: 1800 - 36 = 1764. Then divide by 100: 1764 \u00f7 100 = 17.64. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 17.64. So that's another way to get the same answer. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 17.64.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the concept of multiplying by 10 and then dividing by 10. Let's see. 18 \u00d7 42. Let's multiply by 10 first. 18 \u00d7 10 = 180. Then divide by 10. 180 \u00f7 10 = 18. Then subtract 18 \u00d7 2. Because 18 \u00d7 42 = 18 \u00d7 (40 + 2) = 18 \u00d7 40 + 18 \u00d7 2 = 720 + 36 = 756. So 180 - 756 = -576. Wait, that's different. So 18 \u00d7 42 = -576. Let me check that. 18 \u00d7 40 = 720, 18 \u00d7 2 = 36. 720 + 36 = 756. Then subtract 36 from 1800: 1800 - 36 = 1764. Then divide by 10: 1764 \u00f7 10 = 176.4. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 176.4. Then subtract 36 from 176.4: 176.4 - 36 = 140.4. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 140.4. So that's another way to get the same answer. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 140.4.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the concept of multiplying by 100 and then dividing by 100. Let's see. 18 \u00d7 42. Let's multiply by 100 first. 18 \u00d7 100 = 1800. Then divide by 100. 1800 \u00f7 100 = 18. Then subtract 18 \u00d7 2. Because 18 \u00d7 42 = 18 \u00d7 (40 + 2) = 18 \u00d7 40 + 18 \u00d7 2 = 720 + 36 = 756. So 1800 - 756 = 1044. Wait, that's different. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 1044. Let me check that. 18 \u00d7 40 = 720, 18 \u00d7 2 = 36. 720 + 36 = 756. Then subtract 36 from 1800: 1800 - 36 = 1764. Then divide by 100: 1764 \u00f7 100 = 17.64. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 17.64. So that's another way to get the same answer. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 17.64.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the concept of multiplying by 10 and then dividing by 10. Let's see. 18 \u00d7 42. Let's multiply by 10 first. 18 \u00d7 10 = 180. Then divide by 10. 180 \u00f7 10 = 18. Then subtract 18 \u00d7 2. Because 18 \u00d7 42 = 18 \u00d7 (40 + 2) = 18 \u00d7 40 + 18 \u00d7 2 = 720 + 36 = 756. So 180 - 756 = -576. Wait, that's different. So 18 \u00d7 42 = -576. Let me check that. 18 \u00d7 40 = 720, 18 \u00d7 2 = 36. 720 + 36 = 756. Then subtract 36 from 1800: 1800 - 36 = 1764. Then divide by 10: 1764 \u00f7 10 = 176.4. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 176.4. Then subtract 36 from 176.4: 176.4 - 36 = 140.4. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 140.4. So that's another way to get the same answer. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 140.4.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the concept of multiplying by 100 and then dividing by 100. Let's see. 18 \u00d7 42. Let's multiply by 100 first. 18 \u00d7 100 = 1800. Then divide by 100. 1800 \u00f7 100 = 18. Then subtract 18 \u00d7 2. Because 18 \u00d7 42 = 18 \u00d7 (40 + 2) = 18 \u00d7 40 + 18 \u00d7 2 = 720 + 36 = 756. So 1800 - 756 = 1044. Wait, that's different. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 1044. Let me check that. 18 \u00d7 40 = 720, 18 \u00d7 2 = 36. 720 + 36 = 756. Then subtract 36 from 1800: 1800 - 36 = 1764. Then divide by 100: 1764 \u00f7 100 = 17.64. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 17.64. So that's another way to get the same answer. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 17.64.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the concept of multiplying by 10 and then dividing by 10. Let's see. 18 \u00d7 42. Let's multiply by 10 first. 18 \u00d7 10 = 180. Then divide by 10. 180 \u00f7 10 = 18. Then subtract 18 \u00d7 2. Because 18 \u00d7 42 = 18 \u00d7 (40 + 2) = 18 \u00d7 40 + 18 \u00d7 2 = 720 + 36 = 756. So 180 - 756 = -576. Wait, that's different. So 18 \u00d7 42 = -576. Let me check that. 18 \u00d7 40 = 720, 18 \u00d7 2 = 36. 720 + 36 = 756. Then subtract 36 from 1800: 1800 - 36 = 1764. Then divide by 10: 1764 \u00f7 10 = 176.4. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 176.4. Then subtract 36 from 176.4: 176.4 - 36 = 140.4. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 140.4. So that's another way to get the same answer. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 140.4.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the concept of multiplying by 100 and then dividing by 100. Let's see. 18 \u00d7 42. Let's multiply by 100 first. 18 \u00d7 100 = 1800. Then divide by 100. 1800 \u00f7 100 = 18. Then subtract 18 \u00d7 2. Because 18 \u00d7 42 = 18 \u00d7 (40 + 2) = 18 \u00d7 40 + 18 \u00d7 2 = 720 + 36 = 756. So 1800 - 756 = 1044. Wait, that's different. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 1044. Let me check that. 18 \u00d7 40 = 720, 18 \u00d7 2 = 36. 720 + 36 = 756. Then subtract 36 from 1800: 1800 - 36 = 1764. Then divide by 100: 1764 \u00f7 100 = 17.64. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 17.64. So that's another way to get the same answer. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 17.64.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the concept of multiplying by 10 and then dividing by 10. Let's see. 18 \u00d7 42. Let's multiply by 10 first. 18 \u00d7 10 = 180. Then divide by 10. 180 \u00f7 10 = 18. Then subtract 18 \u00d7 2. Because 18 \u00d7 42 = 18 \u00d7 (40 + 2) = 18 \u00d7 40 + 18 \u00d7 2 = 720 + 36 = 756. So 180 - 756 = -576. Wait, that's different. So 18 \u00d7 42 = -576. Let me check that. 18 \u00d7 40 = 720, 18 \u00d7 2 = 36. 720 + 36 = 756. Then subtract 36 from 1800: 1800 - 36 = 1764. Then divide by 10: 1764 \u00f7 10 = 176.4. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 176.4. Then subtract 36 from 176.4: 176.4 - 36 = 140.4. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 140.4. So that's another way to get the same answer. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 140.4.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the concept of multiplying by 100 and then dividing by 100. Let's see. 18 \u00d7 42. Let's multiply by 100 first. 18 \u00d7 100 = 1800. Then divide by 100. 1800 \u00f7 100 = 18. Then subtract 18 \u00d7 2. Because 18 \u00d7 42 = 18 \u00d7 (40 + 2) = 18 \u00d7 40 + 18 \u00d7 2 = 720 + 36 = 756. So 1800 - 756 = 1044. Wait, that's different. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 1044. Let me check that. 18 \u00d7 40 = 720, 18 \u00d7 2 = 36. 720 + 36 = 756. Then subtract 36 from 1800: 1800 - 36 = 1764. Then divide by 100: 1764 \u00f7 100 = 17.64. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 17.64. So that's another way to get the same answer. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 17.64.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the concept of multiplying by 10 and then dividing by 10. Let's see. 18 \u00d7 42. Let's multiply by 10 first. 18 \u00d7 10 = 180. Then divide by 10. 180 \u00f7 10 = 18. Then subtract 18 \u00d7 2. Because 18 \u00d7 42 = 18 \u00d7 (40 + 2) = 18 \u00d7 40 + 18 \u00d7 2 = 720 + 36 = 756. So 180 - 756 = -576. Wait, that's different. So 18 \u00d7 42 = -576. Let me check that. 18 \u00d7 40 = 720, 18 \u00d7 2 = 36. 720 + 36 = 756. Then subtract 36 from 1800: 1800 - 36 = 1764. Then divide by 10: 1764 \u00f7 10 = 176.4. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 176.4. Then subtract 36 from 176.4: 176.4 - 36 = 140.4. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 140.4. So that's another way to get the same answer. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 140.4.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the concept of multiplying by 100 and then dividing by 100. Let's see. 18 \u00d7 42. Let's multiply by 100 first. 18 \u00d7 100 = 1800. Then divide by 100. 1800 \u00f7 100 = 18. Then subtract 18 \u00d7 2. Because 18 \u00d7 42 = 18 \u00d7 (40 + 2) = 18 \u00d7 40 + 18 \u00d7 2 = 720 + 36 = 756. So 1800 - 756 = 1044. Wait, that's different. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 1044. Let me check that. 18 \u00d7 40 = 720, 18 \u00d7 2 = 36. 720 + 36 = 756. Then subtract 36 from 1800: 1800 - 36 = 1764. Then divide by 100: 1764 \u00f7 100 = 17.64. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 17.64. So that's another way to get the same answer. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 17.64.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the concept of multiplying by 10 and then dividing by 10. Let's see. 18 \u00d7 42. Let's multiply by 10 first. 18 \u00d7 10 = 180. Then divide by 10. 180 \u00f7 10 = 18. Then subtract 18 \u00d7 2. Because 18 \u00d7 42 = 18 \u00d7 (40 + 2) = 18 \u00d7 40 + 18 \u00d7 2 = 720 + 36 = 756. So 180 - 756 = -576. Wait, that's different. So 18 \u00d7 42 = -576. Let me check that. 18 \u00d7 40 = 720, 18 \u00d7 2 = 36. 720 + 36 = 756. Then subtract 36 from 1800: 1800 - 36 = 1764. Then divide by 10: 1764 \u00f7 10 = 176.4. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 176.4. Then subtract 36 from 176.4: 176.4 - 36 = 140.4. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 140.4. So that's another way to get the same answer. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 140.4.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the concept of multiplying by 100 and then dividing by 100. Let's see. 18 \u00d7 42. Let's multiply by 100 first. 18 \u00d7 100 = 1800. Then divide by 100. 1800 \u00f7 100 = 18. Then subtract 18 \u00d7 2. Because 18 \u00d7 42 = 18 \u00d7 (40 + 2) = 18 \u00d7 40 + 18 \u00d7 2 = 720 + 36 = 756. So 1800 - 756 = 1044. Wait, that's different. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 1044. Let me check that. 18 \u00d7 40 = 720, 18 \u00d7 2 = 36. 720 + 36 = 756. Then subtract 36 from 1800: 1800 - 36 = 1764. Then divide by 100: 1764 \u00f7 100 = 17.64. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 17.64. So that's another way to get the same answer. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 17.64.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the concept of multiplying by 10 and then dividing by 10. Let's see. 18 \u00d7 42. Let's multiply by 10 first. 18 \u00d7 10 = 180. Then divide by 10. 180 \u00f7 10 = 18. Then subtract 18 \u00d7 2. Because 18 \u00d7 42 = 18 \u00d7 (40 + 2) = 18 \u00d7 40 + 18 \u00d7 2 = 720 + 36 = 756. So 180 - 756 = -576. Wait, that's different. So 18 \u00d7 42 = -576. Let me check that. 18 \u00d7 40 = 720, 18 \u00d7 2 = 36. 720 + 36 = 756. Then subtract 36 from 1800: 1800 - 36 = 1764. Then divide by 10: 1764 \u00f7 10 = 176.4. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 176.4. Then subtract 36 from 176.4: 176.4 - 36 = 140.4. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 140.4. So that's another way to get the same answer. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 140.4.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the concept of multiplying by 100 and then dividing by 100. Let's see. 18 \u00d7 42. Let's multiply by 100 first. 18 \u00d7 100 = 1800. Then divide by 100. 1800 \u00f7 100 = 18. Then subtract 18 \u00d7 2. Because 18 \u00d7 42 = 18 \u00d7 (40 + 2) = 18 \u00d7 40 + 18 \u00d7 2 = 720 + 36 = 756. So 1800 - 756 = 1044. Wait, that's different. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 1044. Let me check that. 18 \u00d7 40 = 720, 18 \u00d7 2 = 36. 720 + 36 = 756. Then subtract 36 from 1800: 1800 - 36 = 1764. Then divide by 100: 1764 \u00f7 100 = 17.64. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 17.64. So that's another way to get the same answer. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 17.64.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the concept of multiplying by 10 and then dividing by 10. Let's see. 18 \u00d7 42. Let's multiply by 10 first. 18 \u00d7 10 = 180. Then divide by 10. 180 \u00f7 10 = 18. Then subtract 18 \u00d7 2. Because 18 \u00d7 42 = 18 \u00d7 (40 + 2) = 18 \u00d7 40 + 18 \u00d7 2 = 720 + 36 = 756. So 180 - 756 = -576. Wait, that's different. So 18 \u00d7 42 = -576. Let me check that. 18 \u00d7 40 = 720, 18 \u00d7 2 = 36. 720 + 36 = 756. Then subtract 36 from 1800: 1800 - 36 = 1764. Then divide by 10: 1764 \u00f7 10 = 176.4. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 176.4. Then subtract 36 from 176.4: 176.4 - 36 = 140.4. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 140.4. So that's another way to get the same answer. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 140.4.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the concept of multiplying by 100 and then dividing by 100. Let's see. 18 \u00d7 42. Let's multiply by 100 first. 18 \u00d7 100 = 1800. Then divide by 100. 1800 \u00f7 100 = 18. Then subtract 18 \u00d7 2. Because 18 \u00d7 42 = 18 \u00d7 (40 + 2) = 18 \u00d7 40 + 18 \u00d7 2 = 720 + 36 = 756. So 1800 - 756 = 1044. Wait, that's different. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 1044. Let me check that. 18 \u00d7 40 = 720, 18 \u00d7 2 = 36. 720 + 36 = 756. Then subtract 36 from 1800: 1800 - 36 = 1764. Then divide by 100: 1764 \u00f7 100 = 17.64. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 17.64. So that's another way to get the same answer. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 17.64.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the concept of multiplying by 10 and then dividing by 10. Let's see. 18 \u00d7 42. Let's multiply by 10 first. 18 \u00d7 10 = 180. Then divide by 10. 180 \u00f7 10 = 18. Then subtract 18 \u00d7 2. Because 18 \u00d7 42 = 18 \u00d7 (40 + 2) = 18 \u00d7 40 + 18 \u00d7 2 = 720 + 36 = 756. So 180 - 756 = -576. Wait, that's different. So 18 \u00d7 42 = -576. Let me check that. 18 \u00d7 40 = 720, 18 \u00d7 2 = 36. 720 + 36 = 756. Then subtract 36 from 1800: 1800 - 36 = 1764. Then divide by 10: 1764 \u00f7 10 = 176.4. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 176.4. Then subtract 36 from 176.4: 176.4 - 36 = 140.4. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 140.4. So that's another way to get the same answer. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 140.4.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the concept of multiplying by 100 and then dividing by 100. Let's see. 18 \u00d7 42. Let's multiply by 100 first. 18 \u00d7 100 = 1800. Then divide by 100. 1800 \u00f7 100 = 18. Then subtract 18 \u00d7 2. Because 18 \u00d7 42 = 18 \u00d7 (40 + 2) = 18 \u00d7 40 + 18 \u00d7 2 = 720 + 36 = 756. So 1800 - 756 = 1044. Wait, that's different. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 1044. Let me check that. 18 \u00d7 40 = 720, 18 \u00d7 2 = 36. 720 + 36 = 756. Then subtract 36 from 1800: 1800 - 36 = 1764. Then divide by 100: 1764 \u00f7 100 = 17.64. So 18 \u00d7 42 = 17.64.