      "text": "<|begin_of_thought|>\n\nOkay, let's see. I need to find the product of 60 and 31. Hmm, multiplication. Let me think. Multiplying two numbers together. So, 60 times 31. Maybe I can break it down into smaller parts to make it easier. \n\nFirst, I remember that multiplying by 10 is just adding a zero to the end. So, 60 times 31 is the same as 60 times 30 plus 60 times 1. Let me check that. 60 times 30 is 1800, and 60 times 1 is 60. Adding those together gives 1800 + 60 = 1860. Wait, is that right? Let me verify. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method. Let's see, 31 times 60. Well, 31 times 60. Let's break down 31 into 30 and 1. So, 30 times 60 is 1800, and 1 times 60 is 60. Adding those gives 1800 + 60 = 1860. Same result. \n\nBut maybe I can do it step by step. Let's take 60 times 30 first. 60 times 30 is 1800. Then, 60 times 1 is 60. Adding those two gives 1860. \n\nAlternatively, if I use the distributive property, 60 times 31. Let's write 31 as 30 + 1. So, 60 times (30 + 1) = 60 times 30 + 60 times 1. Which is 1800 + 60 = 1860. \n\nWait, that's the same as before. So, either way, the product is 1860. \n\nBut let me check with another method. Let's use the standard multiplication algorithm. \n\n60  \n\u00d7 31  \n------  \nFirst, multiply 60 by 1 (the ones place of 31). That's 60.  \nThen, multiply 60 by 30 (the tens place of 31). That's 1800.  \nNow, add those two results together: 60 + 1800 = 1860. Yep, same answer. \n\nSo, all methods lead to 1860. I think that's solid. \n\nJust to make sure there's no mistake in the calculation. Let's take 60 times 30. 60 times 30 is indeed 1800. Then 60 times 1 is 60. Adding them gives 1860. \n\nAlternatively, if I do 60 times 31, maybe I can write it as 60*(30 + 1) = 60*30 + 60*1 = 1800 + 60 = 1860. Yep, that's correct. \n\nAnother way: 60 times 31. Let's compute 60*30 first, which is 1800. Then 60*1 is 60. Adding them gives 1860. \n\nAlternatively, 60*31. Let's compute 60*30=1800, 60*1=60. 1800+60=1860. \n\nYes, all methods confirm the answer is 1860. \n\nI don't see any errors in the calculations. The key steps are breaking down the numbers into parts, multiplying each part, and then adding the results. \n\nSo, the product of 60 and 31 is 1860. \n\n<|end_of_thought|>\n\n<|begin_of_solution|>\n\nTo find the product of \\(60\\) and \\(31\\), we can use the distributive property of multiplication over addition. Breaking down \\(31\\) into \\(30 + 1\\) allows us to compute:\n\n\\[\n60 \\times 31 = 60 \\times (30 + 1) = 60 \\times 30 + 60 \\times 1\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term separately:\n\n\\[\n60 \\times 30 = 1800 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 60 \\times 1 = 60\n\\]\n\nAdding these results together:\n\n\\[\n1800 + 60 = 1860\n\\]\n\nVerification using another method, such as the standard multiplication algorithm, confirms the result:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n    60 \\\\\n  \\times 31 \\\\\n  \\hline\n   600 \\quad \\text{(60 times 10)} \\\\\n  1800 \\quad \\text{(60 times 30)} \\\\\n  \\hline\n  1860 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of \\(60\\) and \\(31\\) is \\(\\boxed{1860}\\).\n\n<|end_of_solution|>",
