      "text": "<|begin_of_thought|>\n\nOkay, let's see. The question is asking for the product of 87 and 10. Hmm, product means multiplication, right? So I need to multiply 87 by 10. Let me think about how to do this. \n\nFirst, I remember that multiplying by 10 is pretty straightforward. When you multiply any number by 10, you just add a zero to the end of that number. So, if I take 87 and add a zero, that should give me the product. Let me check that. 87 + 0 is still 87. Yeah, that seems right. \n\nWait, maybe I should verify this another way to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Let's break it down. 87 times 10. If I think of 10 as 10 ones, then 87 times 10 would be 87 tens. But 87 tens is the same as 870. So, 87 tens is 870. That matches what I got when I added a zero. \n\nAlternatively, I can use the distributive property. 87 times 10 can be written as 80 times 10 plus 7 times 10. 80 times 10 is 800, and 7 times 10 is 70. Adding those together gives 800 + 70 = 870. Yep, that also checks out. \n\nAnother way to think about it is that multiplying by 10 is equivalent to shifting the decimal point one place to the right. Since 87 is a whole number, multiplying by 10 doesn't change its value. So, 87.0 becomes 870. That's another way to confirm the answer. \n\nI don't think I made any mistakes here. All the methods I tried lead to the same result. Let me just recap: 87 times 10 is 870. I can't see any errors in my reasoning. So, the product should be 870.\n\n<|end_of_thought|>\n\n<|begin_of_solution|>\n\nThe product of 87 and 10 is calculated by multiplying 87 by 10. Since multiplying by 10 adds a zero to the end of the number, the result is \\( 87 \\times 10 = 870 \\).\n\n\\boxed{870}\n\n<|end_of_solution|>",
